gn=\"top\" width=\"71\"> \n\n</p>\n </td> <td style=\"width: 35.4pt; border-top: none; border-left: none; border-bottom: solid black 1.0pt; border-right: solid black 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid black .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid black .5pt; mso-border-alt: solid black .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 5.15pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"71\"> \n\n</p>\n </td> <td style=\"width: 1.0cm; border-top: none; border-left: none; border-bottom: solid black 1.0pt; border-right: solid black 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid black .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid black .5pt; mso-border-alt: solid black .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 5.15pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"57\"> \n\n</p>\n </td> <td style=\"width: 35.45pt; border-top: none; border-left: none; border-bottom: solid black 1.0pt; border-right: solid black 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid black .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid black .5pt; mso-border-alt: solid black .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 5.15pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"71\"> \n\n</p>\n </td> <td style=\"width: 35.45pt; border-top: none; border-left: none; border-bottom: solid black 1.0pt; border-right: solid black 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid black .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid black .5pt; mso-border-alt: solid black .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 5.15pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"71\"> \n\n</p>\n </td> <td style=\"width: 35.45pt; border-top: none; border-left: none; border-bottom: solid black 1.0pt; border-right: solid black 1.0pt; mso-border-top-alt: solid black .5pt; mso-border-left-alt: solid black .5pt; mso-border-alt: solid black .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 5.15pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"71\"> \n\n</p>\n </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> \n\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>T ?œ cnicas recomendadas de presentaci ? ?‚n y evaluaci ? ?‚n.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<span>Se recomienda a los instructores presentar la exposici ? ?‚n te ? ?‚rica de este tema junto con materiales relacionados con el curso en el aula. En consecuencia, se mostrar ? n ejemplos apropiados de evaluaciones. Durante la impartici ? ?‚n de la conferencia podr ? n ser preferibles ejercicios en clase, estudios de casos y trabajos en grupo relacionados con la evaluaci ? ?‚n.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p>\n<span>Se podr ?œ an utilizar ex ? menes escritos u orales sobre c ? ?‚mo alentar y recopilar comentarios de los alumnos. Tambi ?œ n se sugiere que se utilicen actividades pr ? cticas como la aplicaci ? ?‚n de los resultados de la evaluaci ? ?‚n, presentaciones grupales y tareas para evaluar el desempe ? o de los alumnos. Un ensayo tambi ?œ n es una posible herramienta para evaluar la capacidad de un alumno para resolver un problema m ? s complejo</span>\n</p>',NULL,'text',NULL,32767,0,'2026-04-19 03:45:28',1),(378,147,'7. Diseño del curso','</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>7.1. Reconocer los factores a considerar al dise ? ar un programa de aprendizaje.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Detalles del tema</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<span>El objetivo de esta parte es permitir a los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n identificar los factores que tendr ? n un impacto en el dise ? o de un programa de aprendizaje. El instructor en formaci ? ?‚n debe presentar c ? ?‚mo relacionar los resultados del aprendizaje de un programa espec ?œ fico con la competencia especificada.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 115%;\">\n<span>Al dise ? ar un nuevo curso para gente de mar en formaci ? ?‚n, el instructor en formaci ? ?‚n deber ?œ a identificar las competencias espec ?œ ficas requeridas por el Convenio y el C ? ?‚digo STCW. Los resultados del aprendizaje son par ? metros esenciales para demostrar estas competencias. Los resultados planificados de este curso modelo deben ser coherentes con la competencia identificada en el Convenio y C ? ?‚digo STCW. Por lo tanto, los contenidos did ? cticos deben cubrir todos los conocimientos enumerados en el Convenio y C ? ?‚digo STCW y tambi ?œ n se deben seleccionar los medios did ? cticos adecuados en consecuencia para facilitar el desarrollo de competencias de los alumnos.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<span>Actividades 7.1.1 - Relacionar los resultados con la competencia identificada</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<span>Esta actividad tiene como objetivo permitir a los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n conectar los resultados del aprendizaje con una competencia espec ?œ fica. En esta actividad, se recomienda a los instructores demostrar las diferencias entre las competencias y los resultados. Los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n podr ? n identificar la distinci ? ?‚n clave entre resultados de aprendizaje y competencia. Se sugiere que se proporcionen las tablas de competencias del Convenio y C ? ?‚digo STCW para indicar la conexi ? ?‚n entre competencia y resultados del aprendizaje.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<span>Actividades 7.1.2 - Determinar los resultados de un programa espec ?œ fico</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 115%;\">\n<span>Esta actividad tiene como objetivo permitir a los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n desarrollar resultados de aprendizaje relevantes. Se sugiere que los instructores introduzcan los principios y reglas b ? sicos en forma de ejercicios en clase que entren en la categor ?œ a de contenido, estrategias de ense ? anza y evaluaci ? ?‚n para un programa de aprendizaje espec ?œ fico. Es mejor que los instructores proporcionen una muestra de la lecci ? ?‚n sobre el desarrollo de los resultados del aprendizaje y concluyan con algunos consejos para ayudar a los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n a terminar su trabajo.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>T ?œ cnicas recomendadas de presentaci ? ?‚n y evaluaci ? ?‚n.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<span>Se propone equipar el aula con material did ? ctico como computadora, proyector, software aplicable y videos. Se podr ?œ a utilizar un nuevo curso equipado con un programa de ense ? anza, material did ? ctico y resultados de ense ? anza para explicar y simular el proceso de dise ? o del curso para los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 115%;\">\n<span>Se sugiere que las tareas grupales escritas u orales relativas al desarrollo de un programa de aprendizaje modular con una competencia designada elegida del Convenio y C ? ?‚digo STCW se utilicen para evaluar el desempe ? o de los alumnos a este respecto. Ser ?œ a mejor que los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n entregaran comentarios escritos o comentarios orales al final del curso.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>7.2. Desarrollar un nuevo curso</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Detalles del tema</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<span>El objetivo de este tema es permitir a los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n desarrollar un nuevo curso de formaci ? ?‚n. Se deber ?œ a informar a los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n que los cursos modelo de la OMI podr ?œ an utilizarse como herramientas ? tiles en su proceso de desarrollo de cursos porque el desarrollo de cursos modelo se basa en los resultados basados en competencias del Convenio y C ? ?‚digo STCW. Una vez completadas las competencias selectivas y los correspondientes resultados planificados, tambi ?œ n se deben considerar otros factores antes de desarrollar un programa de aprendizaje. Si el instructor est ? familiarizado con los antecedentes detallados de los alumnos y sus necesidades de conocimientos te ? ?‚ricos y habilidades pr ? cticas, el programa de aprendizaje ser ? m ? s aplicable y efectivo.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<span>Actividades 7.2.1 - Utilizar los cursos modelo de la OMI</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 115%;\">\n<span>Esta actividad tiene como objetivo permitir a los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n utilizar adecuadamente los cursos modelo de la OMI durante el proceso de dise ? o del curso. Se sugiere que el instructor presente los prop ? ?‚sitos y principios de los cursos modelo de la OMI con ejemplos para ilustrar c ? ?‚mo funcionan en el dise ? o del curso.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<span>Actividades 7.2.2 - Desarrollar un programa de aprendizaje que se adapte a las necesidades de los alumnos</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<span>Esta actividad tiene como objetivo demostrar la capacidad de los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n para desarrollar un programa de aprendizaje relevante para las necesidades espec ?œ ficas del alumno. Se recomienda a los instructores resumir todo el proceso de dise ? o del curso bas ? ndose en el conocimiento previamente obtenido por los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n. Los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n deben desarrollar un programa de estudios del curso con referencia a la plantilla del dise ? o del curso con respecto a los factores clave como los resultados del aprendizaje, las actividades y la evaluaci ? ?‚n, etc.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>T ?œ cnicas recomendadas de presentaci ? ?‚n y evaluaci ? ?‚n.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%; border: none; mso-padding-alt: 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt 31.0pt; mso-border-shadow: yes;\">\n<span>Las discusiones en grupo o la lluvia de ideas podr ?œ an utilizarse como m ?œ todo de ense ? anza apropiado para presentar este tema. Se sugiere que los cursos modelo de la OMI podr ?œ an utilizarse como herramienta para promover la eficacia de la ense ? anza. Se recomienda a los instructores que entreguen una muestra espec ?œ fica en detalle, por ejemplo, los materiales relacionados con el proceso de dise ? o de un nuevo curso frente a los instructores en formaci ? ?‚n.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 115%;\">\n<span>Se sugiere que las tareas grupales escritas u orales podr ?œ an realizarse bajo la gu ?œ a de un curso modelo espec ?œ fico de la OMI. Se recomienda que los instructores puedan entregar comentarios escritos o comentarios orales al final del curso.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>',NULL,'text',NULL,32767,0,'2026-04-19 03:45:28',1),(383,151,'1.1. Safey guidance','<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The STCW Conference 1978 recognized the need to promote technical knowledge, skills and professionalism of seafarers. Resolution 8 of the Conference notes the role played by the Convention as revised on improving the preparation of marine, but also recommends the recruitment with the highest degree of professionalism; encourages training new officers and the adoption of measures that raise pride in their work and professionalism of the staff employed by the companies. Some of these ideas may perhaps sound somewhat outdated now, but to create a safety culture must start from the professionalism of seafarers. This culture is not just supposed to prevent accidents or reduce them, even though they, in general, appear to be the best measures of success for safety.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The operations carried out by ships, the ability to do the right thing at the right time, both routine and emergency operations, is part of the training required to achieve the levels required by the STCW Convention. The quality and effectiveness of this training will play a key role in determining the attitude and way of doing things, the professionalism of the sailors in their work. Furthermore, the attitude adopted by the Navy will turn largely shaped by the \"culture\" of the company. It is relatively rare to occur on board new types of injuries and many who continue to occur due to non-marine insurance behaviors. These errors, or more often, best practices or violations of established rules, can be easily avoided. Those who commit them are often conscious with their own mistakes. They may have taken \"shortcuts\" that should have been avoided. Most of them have received training aimed at preventing these behaviors. However, accidents continue to occur because there is a culture that tolerates \"calculated risk\".</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The challenge for trainers and the training itself, and to the heads of the company and the ship is to minimize these unsafe behaviors, transmit skills and especially the attitudes necessary to ensure that security objectives are met marked. The aim should be to instill self-marine strong and effective regulation and encouraging them to take ownership of good practices as their own. The principles of internationally recognized safeguards security and industry best practices should become an integral part of the criteria of each individual.Chapter VIII of the STCW on the principles to be considered in the guard, contains a good example of the Convention\'s contribution to the development of a safety culture.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>1.2. Principles of survival at sea.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>When a vessel at sea has to be abandoned, it is to be done suddenly without any time for thinking. One is directly exposed to the elements and all efforts are focused on one ? s own survival first and then those of ship-mates. In such a situation, where the dangers are many and of varying degrees, the best weapon for combating these dangers is the will to survive. There are many incidents where people survived for more than 30 days due to will power, more than anything else.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>However, every seaman will have to know and be familiar with all means provided to him on board a ship for survival at sea. He should also prepare himself mentally for all types of emergencies as a consequence of which the ship may have to be abandoned. In this course, we will get to know:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l6 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The various life-saving appliances on board a ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l6 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The procedures for training.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l6 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The types of emergency situation that may develop onboard a ship. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l6 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The action that one should be prepared to take during such situation and, also.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l6 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">e.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The actions necessary to survive when in the survival craft.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>As pre-abandonment preparation, four guidelines for survival at sea are most important. These should always be kept in mind: </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Regular training.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Preparedness for any emergency.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Knowledge of action to be taken, and</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Knowledge of main dangers to survival.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">e.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>When called to survival craft station.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">f.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>When required to abandon ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">g.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>When in the water.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">h.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>When aboard a survival craft.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 71.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">i.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Knowledge of the main dangers to survivors.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Whenever drills are carried out, you should participate in them wholeheartedly. In addition, you should also see how your ship-mates are behaving and help them if necessary. Team work is the most important. Therefore, there should be much understanding among all the persons. Even during working hours or during leisure time, one must be aware of existing conditions on the ship. In other words, one should always take an active part in all activities concerning safety of the ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>1.3. Types of life-saving appliances normally carried on ships.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Definitions:</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l12 level1 lfo5;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Survival Craft is a craft that can save the lives of persons in distress from the time of abandoning the ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l12 level1 lfo5;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Rescue Boat is a boat designed to rescue persons in distress and to Marshall Survival craft.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l12 level1 lfo5;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Float-free Launching is the method of launching a survival craft so that the craft is automatically releases from a sinking ship and is ready for use.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l12 level1 lfo5;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Free Fall Launching is the method of launching a survival craft where by the craft with its compliment of persons and equipment ? s onboard is released and allowed to fall into the sea without any restraint.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l12 level1 lfo5;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">5.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Immersion Suit is a protective suit which reduces the body heat-loss of a person wearing it in cold water.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l12 level1 lfo5;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">6.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Inflatable Appliance is one which depends on non-rigid gas filled chambers of buoyancy and which is normally kept un-inflated until ready for use.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l12 level1 lfo5;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">7.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Thermal Protective Aid is a bag or suit made of water proof material with low thermal conductivity.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l12 level1 lfo5;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">8.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Launching Appliances or arrangements is a means of transferring survival craft or rescue boat from its stowed position safely to the water.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Fire-Fighting Appliances </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>As laid down by the rules, every ship is provided with sufficient numbers and types of equipment have to combat all fires before they develop into major ones. It is the responsibility of all ships board personnel to not only take part in all drills but also familiarize themselves with the usage of such equipment, especially in their own work areas.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Personal Life-Saving Appliances </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Similar to FFA each ship is well provided with appliances and survival craft for saving of all lives onboard in case the ship has to be abandoned. The same principles as in the FFA apply here also LSA / FFA plan provided on the ship and posted in alleyways gives the layout of all LSA/FFA on board the vessel or ship. LSA operation instructions need to be posted underneath the emergency lights.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Survival Craft </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Life-Boats </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>One may come across different types of life-boats on different ships as:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Open.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Partially enclosed.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Self-righting partially enclosed.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Totally enclosed.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">e.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Totally enclosed with air support system.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">f.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Totally enclosed with fire protection system.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">g.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Totally enclosed free fall life boats.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>For passenger ships, the capacity of the life-boats is generally sufficient for every person on board. For cargo ship, the capacity is generally twice the number of persons onboard. Life-boats are either launched by davits or by free-fall method. Embarkation of the boats may be done either at the embarkation deck or through ladders when the boat is in the water. Nowadays, it is possible for all personnel, on a cargo ship to embark a life-boat and leave the ship. This means that there need not be any person left on the ship to lower a life boat.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Life boats davit falls (wire) to be renewed every 5 year and end to end changed after 2.5 years.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Life ? Rafts </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Life rafts are inflatable. They can either be manually launched or launched by davits or they can also automatically float-free from a sinking ship. Life Rafts &amp; Hydrostatic release units to be serviced once a year by an approved shore service station.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Rescue Boats </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Depending on the types of ship (passenger / cargo) a minimum number of rescue boats have to be carried. A life-boat may be classed as a rescue boat also, if it conforms to requirements of a rescue boat.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Personal Life Saving Appliances </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Life Buoys </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>According to its size each ship should carry a specific number of life-buoys distributed on the deck. Some of these life-buoys should have life lines, self-igniting lights or smoke floats, retro reflective tapes, ship ? s name and port of registry to be clearly marked on all life buoys.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Life Jackets </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Life jackets of approved type may be rigid or inflatable. For every ship, the number of life-jackets to be carried will depend on the maximum number of persons onboard that ship at any particular time. A whistle is to be firmly secured to each life-jacket. Each life jacket will require having a fixed or flashing light also a retro- reflective tape. Port of Registry and Ship ? s Name need to be marked clearly on all life jackets.</span>\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Immersion Suits and Thermal Proactive Aids </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>These are provided for at least 3 persons in each non-enclosed life-boat on both passenger and cargo ships. If immersion suits are provided there is no need of thermal provided aids.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>1.4. Introduction of SOLAS training manual.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The word SOLAS is an abbreviation and SOLAS full form is ? Safety Of Life At Sea ? , an international maritime treaty, also known as SOLAS Convention or International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea (SOLAS), which establishes the least safety measures in the construction, equipment and operation of merchant ships.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>IMO SOLAS 74, the last adopted revised convention of 1974, includes a number of regulations under different SOLAS chapters, which deals with safety precautions and safety procedures starting from the construction of the ship to real emergency like ? ? Abandon Ship ? . ? The convention is updated to meet the safety norms in the modern shipping industry from time to time.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>This article explains the contents of SOLAS chapters and regulations providing a summary of SOLAS, i.e. different chapters of SOLAS and the regulations they carry. Marine Insight has provided links of various articles which will help the readers to understand how the regulation of the SOLAS Annexes is implemented on a seagoing vessel and the importance of SOLAS. The International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea (SOLAS), 1974 describes the requirement for all merchant ship of any flag state to comply with the minimum safety norms laid down in the chapters.</span>\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The SOLAS Training manual is a plan to provide guidance on the use of all lifesaving appliances onboard.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>SOLAS Chapter III requires that all ships should be provided with a SOLAS Training Manual or Safety Training Manual detailing all training on the safety aspects of the ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>How do we make the SOLAS Training Manual:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>This Plan is developed in line with IMO Requirements and common marine practice.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>It is approval by the Administration or a Recognized Organization (RO).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>It is inspected by the Port State Control (PSC).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The training manuals shall contain following:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l14 level1 lfo8;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Instructions and information on the life-saving appliances provided on the ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l14 level1 lfo8;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Information on the best methods of survival.</span>\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The manual shall be:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l11 level1 lfo9;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>In language understood by the crew.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l11 level1 lfo9;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>In easy to understand text with pictures.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l11 level1 lfo9;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>It could be one or more volumes.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l11 level1 lfo9;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>It could be in the form of audio-visual aids as an alternative to printed material.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l11 level1 lfo9;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">e.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Depending on the lifesaving appliances provided in the ship, the following shall be explained in detail:</span>\n</p>\n\ni.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Donning of following:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Lifejackets.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Immersion suits.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Anti-exposure suits.</span>\n</p>\n\nii.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Muster at the assigned stations</span>\n</p>\n\niii.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Survival Craft:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Boarding.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Launching.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Clearing.</span>\n</p>\n\niv.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Method of launching from within the survival craft-on load/off load.</span>\n</p>\n\nv.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Release from launching appliances.</span>\n</p>\n\nvi.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Methods and use of devices for protection in launching areas.</span>\n</p>\n\nvii.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Illumination in launching areas.</span>\n</p>\n\nviii.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Use the following:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>All survival equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Detection equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Radio life-saving appliances.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Sea anchors.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Engine and accessories.</span>\n</p>\n\nix.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Survival craft:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Recovery.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Stowage.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Securing.</span>\n</p>\n\nx.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Hazards of exposure and the need for warm clothing.</span>\n</p>\n\nxi.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Best use of the survival craft facilities in order to survive.</span>\n</p>\n\nxii.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Methods of retrieval, including the use of helicopter rescue gear including:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Slings.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Baskets.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Stretchers.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Breeches buoy.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Shore life-saving apparatus.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Ship ? ? s line-throwing apparatus.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 90.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Other functions contained in the muster list and emergency instructions.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The Index of a Training manual contains following:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo15;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>SOLAS Requirements.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo15;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Personal Life Saving Equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo15;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Muster List and Emergency Instructions.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo15;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Life Boats and Rescue Boats.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo15;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">e.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Survival Equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo15;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">f.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Expose Hazards.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo15;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">g.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Use of Survival Craft.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo15;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">h.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Methods of Retrieval.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo15;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">i.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Emergency Repair of Life Saving Appliances.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo15;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">j.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Safety Equipment Inventory.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 18.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l13 level2 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.5<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>. Safety symbols related to survival.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Regulations</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 1.0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -14.15pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level2 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>IMO symbols with text</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Regulations: SOLAS, Chapter III, Regulation 20.10 requires signs to identify the locations of life saving equipment in accordance with recommendations of IMO.<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes;\"> ? </span>Reference should be made to the symbols related to Life Saving Appliances and arrangements adopted by IMO by resolution A.760 (18), MSC82 (70) and A.952 (23). </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The standard IMO designs may be used without text. This format will be preferred where English is not the first language of the passengers and crew.</span>\n</p>\n\n<img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image.png\">\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%281%29.png\">\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 1.0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -14.15pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level2 lfo1;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">B.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>IMO symbols without text</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Regulations: SOLAS, Chapter III, Regulation 20.10 requires signs to identify the locations of life saving equipment in accordance with recommendations of IMO.<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes;\"> ? </span>Reference should be made to the symbols related to Life Saving Appliances and arrangements adopted by IMO by resolution A.760 (18), MSC82 (70) and A.952 (23). </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 1.0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -14.15pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level2 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>IMO symbols without text</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Regulations: SOLAS, Chapter III, Regulation 20.10 requires signs to identify the locations of life saving equipment in accordance with recommendations of IMO.<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes;\"> ? </span>Reference should be made to the symbols related to Life Saving Appliances and arrangements adopted by IMO by resolution A.760 (18), MSC82 (70) and A.952 (23). </span>\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 1.0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -14.15pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level2 lfo1;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">C.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Safety signs</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Regulations: Safety signs are required to complement or expand the scope of officially designated signage. Many of these signs will be required by Flag State and Classification Authorities to cover safety and lifesaving equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%282%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 1.0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -14.15pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level2 lfo1;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">D.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Direction signs</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Regulations: SOLAS 1974 Chapter III Regulation 11.5, Chapter II-2 Regulation 13.1.3 and Regulation 13.3.2.5.1 require all escape routes to be marked with photo luminescent signs or signs illuminated by emergency light or both.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%283%29.png\">\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 1.0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -14.15pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level2 lfo1;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">E.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Fire Control Symbols</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Regulations: Although not required by regulations, many vessels use signs depicting Fire Plan symbols to identify equipment onboard. Currently there exist two sets of Fire Plan symbols. SOLAS Consolidated edition 2001 Chapter II-2 regulation 20 and SOLAS amendments 2000 Chapter II-2 regulation 24 requires the use of fire control symbols on Fire Plans in accordance with IMO Resolution A.654(16), available as signs.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%284%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 1.0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -14.15pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level2 lfo1;\">\n<span style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\"><span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"><span><strong>F</strong></span></span><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Fire Signs</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 1.0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -14.15pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level2 lfo1;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%285%29.png\"></span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Emergency situations and mustering</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Shipboard operations that are generally not routine may be divided into:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Special Operations</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Critical Operations</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Emergencies</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Special Operations are those where errors may become apparent only after they have created hazardous situations or when accidents have occurred:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Navigations with Pilot embarked.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Bunkering operations.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Loading / Discharging.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Heavy Weather operations.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Critical Operations are those where an error may immediately cause an accident or a situation, which could threaten personnel, the environment or the vessel.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Navigation in dense fog, heavy weather or in restricted waters.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Loading / Discharging dangerous goods.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Working aloft.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Critical machinery operation.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">5.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Helicopter operations.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>An Emergency is a situation where there is an immediate danger to personnel, the asset (vessel and cargo) or the environment. To deal with such emergencies, shipboard contingency plans are established and frequent training conducted to familiarize the crew.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>It is important to note that any mishap occurring during a special operation or a critical operation can result in an emergency situation.</span>\n</p>',NULL,'text',NULL,32767,0,'2026-04-19 03:45:28',1),(384,151,'2.1.	Types of emergencies.','<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Emergency may happen at any time; it has no date or time to occur. Persons should always be prepared at all times to meet such types of contingencies/emergencies. Types of emergencies on board ships include, but not limited to the following:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Fire, Explosion.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Abandoning ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%281%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Man Overboard.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%282%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%283%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Collision, Flooding, Stranding, Foundering.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%284%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">e<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Shifting of cargo (listing of Ship).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%285%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">f.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Person trapped in enclosed space.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">g.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Engine failure.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">h.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--> <span>Structural Failure.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%286%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">i.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--> <span>Oil spill.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%287%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">j.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Serious personal injury, which can risk life.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">k.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Adverse reaction of dangerous goods or hazardous bulk materials.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Before the situation goes out of control, the proper and timely action taken will prevent emergencies. In case of fire, it may be necessary to keep some or all of the survival crafts ready for immediate use while the fire fighting continues.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>PRECAUTIONS:</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Although emergencies may take place in any situation, survival depends largely on emergency preparedness. Listed below are the general precautions that are followed on board to prevent occurrence of emergencies and to react and respond correctly should an emergency occur. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>General precautions taken against such emergencies include the following:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Contingency plans prepared &amp; exercised during the emergency drills.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Safe working practices followed strictly at all times &amp; during all operations.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>All rules or regulations concerning safety &amp; environmental protection complied with.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Operational precautions taken as necessary during various operations or activities onboard. Specific emergency will require specific precautions and specific response as listed in contingency manual.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>FIRE PROVISIONS:</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>In general, the means provided to combat fire include the following:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>A fire can be extinguished by removing any element of the Fire-quadrangle i.e., by removing ignition or heat (cooling method) or air (smothering method) or fuel (starving method) or chain reaction. Any one or a combination of the above methods is used. Equipment mainly include fire extinguishers, fire hoses with nozzles, CO2 or inert gas or foam flooding system, SW sprinkler system, foam applicators, sand, fireman ? s outfits/ smoke suits with helmets, SCBA sets, fire axes, etc.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>FOUNDERING:</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The term Foundering means ? Going Under ? and specifically relates to sinking of a vessel. The means provided on board to prevent such an occurrence mainly include:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Watertight doors and other closing devices which maintain the watertight integrity of a ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>High structural strength to prevent structural failures that may lead to foundering of the whole ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Proper maintenance and frequent inspections of various critical equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Safety of Navigation.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>CREW EXPERTISE:</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The effectiveness of the means provided onboard for combating emergencies depends on the expertise of the personnel. The expertise of the personnel will depend on their training.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.2.<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1;\"> ? ? ? ? ? </span>Muster list and emergency signals.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Muster List:</span></strong><span> The muster list shall be prepared by the master of the ship (actually prepared by one of the deck officers/ safety officers under the guidance/ approval of the master) before the ship proceeds to sea, and format of the muster list for a passenger ship must be approved by the government and it must be kept revised at all times and is necessary if more than 25% of the crew changes. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%288%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The muster list shall specify:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The muster list shall show all the duties assigned to the different members of the crew against their name and rank to ensure:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Closing of watertight doors, fire doors, valves, scuppers, vent pipes, side scuttles, skylights, portholes and other similar openings. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Equipping&amp; making ready the survival crafts.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Mustering of crew &amp; passengers and correct donning of lifejackets. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The muster list shall show all the special duties for fighting the fire, with regard to the ship ? s fire plans, such as managing fire parties, firefighting equipment etc. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Carrying of emergency radio, EPIRB, SART and other devices/ equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Ship ? s pyrotechnics. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">5.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Emergency signal is sounded. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">6.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Abandon-ship signal / order are understood.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Copies of the muster list must be posted on conspicuous places, including the navigating bridge, crew accommodation and engine room.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>In passenger ships, the list shall show the location of passenger muster stations. Usually these will be the covered alleyways or public rooms, so as to protect passengers from the weather. Illustrations and instructions are to be posted in the passengers ? cabins, at muster stations and other public/ common spaces, informing them of their muster stations, and procedure of donning the lifejackets.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>General Emergency Alarm Signal: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>It is a signal for summoning crew and passengers, whenever any emergency occurs, to their muster stations. It consists of at-least seven short blasts followed by a long blast on the ship ? s whistle or siren and additionally on an electrically operated bell or Kinston or other equipment warning systems. Emergency signal, which are powered by the ship ? s main power supply must also be provided with the emergency sources of electrical power, in case the ship ? s power fails. The system shall be capable of operating from the navigating bridge (except for the ships whistle) and from other strategic points. It shall be audible through all the working places - engine room, deck and the accommodation. On hearing the emergency signal, all persons must immediately go to their muster stations as per the muster list. They must wear their warm clothing or water proof clothing or extra clothing and collect/don their lifejackets, have a good drink of water and swing into action to save the ship, by way of controlling the situation and preparing the life-saving appliances.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Abandon-ship Signal: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>There is no statutory <strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\">ABANDONSHIP SIGNAL</strong>. It is to be given verbally and through a public address system by the Master of the vessel only. It may differ from ship to ship and company to company. Before giving abandonment order, firefighting and damage control party, wheelhouse party &amp; engine room party must be called-off; final report of preparedness must be made by each lifeboat to the bridge. Then, the Master of the vessel gives the order, to man the boats and clear the ship ? s side and steam/ row away from the danger area to a safer distance about 0.25 nautical miles.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%289%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Emergency Drills: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Everyone thinks that they will never to abandon ship and most people never have to, but emergencies do occur when they are least expected. It is absolutely essential to know what to do and how to do it. For that, knowledge of L.S.A. and F.F.A. equipment of the ship you have joined is a must. So that in an emergency you can use one quickly and effectively, make a habit of always taking a long drink of fresh water whenever the signal for lifeboat drill, abandon ship drill or emergency stations is sounded. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2810%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>You must read the muster list on joining your vessel and must act accordingly. Before abandoning ship, its necessary to have some extra gears put in the boats such as emergency radio, EPIRB, SART, blankets, tinned food, biscuits, notebooks, pencils, extra fuel, boat charts etc. Never jump in a hurry if left onboard; lower your lifeboat or life raft if possible. Remember that your ship is number one lifeboat. Make the best possible use of the ship ? s equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Many lives have been lost by premature and unnecessary abandonment of ships. Never jump without lifejackets. Always try to keep dry even after boarding the lifeboat or life-raft. Do not try to swim unnecessarily, the vital energy/ heat is lost which assists hypothermia to set in. Wet clothes are better than no clothing, wring out top layer of water and wear them again as quickly as possible.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.3.<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1;\"> ? ? ? ? ? </span>Value of crew training and drill.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>CREW &amp; EMERGENCY INSTRUCTIONS: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>After joining a ship, as soon as possible the personnel should acquire knowledge of:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The meaning of emergency signals.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Instructions on the muster list &amp; their duties.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The location and use of life-saving equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The location and use of fire-fighting equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">e.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Escape routes and equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">f.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Emergencies involving the sinking of the ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">g.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The means provided for survival on ship and survival craft.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2811%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>EXTRA EQUIPMENT AND SURVIVAL: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>When abandoning ship, if time permits, the personnel should take the following extra equipment into the survival craft.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Blankets &amp; other woollens, provisions, other radio equipment, other pyrotechnics, etc.</span>\n</p>',NULL,'text',NULL,32767,0,'2026-04-19 03:45:28',1),(385,151,'3. EVACUATION','<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>EVACUATION</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level2 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Abandoning ship ? last resort.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>When all efforts to save the stricken vessel prove to be unsuccessful, the ship has to be abandoned. The ship usually offers the best chance of survival and that abandoning ship should only be undertaken if all other measures fail. Never leave your vessel until it leaves you. Your ship is the safest lifeboat. Never jump in panic. Good organization and training will help to ensure that available time is used to its best effect and that abandoning the vessel is carried out in as safe a manner as possible and without panic, maintaining complete discipline, silence, strict adherence to orders and immediately; controlling any evidence of panic, using force if necessary. All members of the crew and passengers will be required to exercise self-control, courage and usefulness. Failure to observe all these facts may result in unnecessary losses of life. Public address system should be fully utilized. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The last person to abandon the vessel will be the master and those who engage in controlling the fire, radio officer, chief engineer and that no one must be left onboard behind. Before leaving the vessel, all machinery should be stopped and watertight doors and hatches should be tightly closed. When ordered to man the lifeboats and life-rafts, they should be lowered with as many people aboard as possible and try to lower all life boats and life rafts and should then quickly clear the ship side and lie off ready to embark the remaining complement from the water if any. This will avoid a dangerous waiting period alongside the vessel. Except in rough weather the lifeboats and life-rafts should be secured together and towed well clear of the wreck area to pick up survivors if any in the water, swimmers should group themselves together and support each offer till the rescue boat corners to pick up. Swimmers must move away from the ship as quickly as possible to avoid violent local suction created by the sinking ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Remember </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>NO SHIP IS TO BE ABANDONED, EXCEPT BY THE ORDER OF THE MASTER. Apart from the general emergency alarm signal, the master of the vessel will designate a special signal/ verbal order for ? ABANDON SHIP ? . NOBODY IS A SURVIVOR TILL ONE IS RESCUED SAFELY.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level2 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Abandoning ship - complications.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>ABANDONING SHIP COMPLICATIONS: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The complications in abandoning ship are caused by;</span>\n</p>\n\n<ol style=\"margin-top: 0cm;\" start=\"1\" type=\"a\">\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span>Some of the survival crafts not capable of being launched due to adverse list or trim acquired by vessel, fire or explosion causing damage to survival craft, heavy weather.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span>Absence of sufficient lighting.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span>Absence of personnel assigned to certain duties.</span>\n</li>\n\n</ol>\n\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level2 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Personal preparation for abandoning ship</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>PERSONAL PREPARATIONS: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Following are the preparations one should make for abandoning ship:</span>\n</p>\n\n<ul style=\"margin-top: 0cm;\" type=\"square\">\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo3;\">\n<span>Take all the equipment as per the duties in the muster cards.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo3;\">\n<span>Take extra provisions &amp; blankets.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo3;\">\n<span>Wear sufficient woollens &amp; clothing for protection against body heat loss.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo3;\">\n<span>Wear lifejacket/ immersion suit/ thermal protective aid.</span>\n</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>DO NOT JUMP IN WATER,</span></strong><span> however if you have to, remember: </span>\n</p>\n\n<ul style=\"margin-top: 0cm;\" type=\"square\">\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo4;\">\n<span>Have your lifejacket well secured and hold it down by crossing the arms over the chest blocking of the nose with one hand. </span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo4;\">\n<span>Make sure that everything is clear in the water, such as any floating object etc. </span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo4;\">\n<span>Draw attention of the lifeboat or life-raft, if any nearby, with whistle. </span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo4;\">\n<span>Never dive, keep feet together, look straight ahead and cross legs while jumping.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo4;\">\n<span>Swim on your backstroke. Try to board the lifeboat or life raft as soon as possible. Never swim for longer periods.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo4;\">\n<span>Try to jump from the lowest deck as possible, never jump from more than 4.5 to 6 meters height.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo4;\">\n<span>As far as possible, avoid jumping into water. Try to board survival craft without getting into the water by ladders, lifeline, water hose and scramble nets or other available means.</span>\n</li>\n\n</ul>\n\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level2 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Need to prevent panic</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>One should not panic in case of any emergency, rather should compose others who start to panic. Members of crew or passengers getting panic can cause distress not only to themselves but to others as well making emergencies to grow exponentially.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level2 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.5.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Crew duties to passengers.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Duties of crew with respect to passengers are:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Ensure all passengers onboard are present at their muster station.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Nobody is missing Injured passengers are taken care off with proper first aid</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Female passengers, children and infirm passengers should be managed carefully.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Details of persons who have declared a need for special care or assistance in emergency situations shall be recorded and communicated to the Master prior departure. In addition, the names &amp; gender of all persons on board, distinguishing between adults, children &amp; infants shall be recorded for search &amp; rescue purposes. This information shall be kept ashore and made readily available to search &amp; rescue services when needed.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Duties of crew with respect to passengers in case of emergency are:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Printed emergency plans should be prepared &amp; complied with. Drills as required by the SOLAS should be conducted; passengers shall be strongly encouraged to attend these drills. The passengers should be made familiar as soon as possible after coming onboard the ship. Theyshould be guided to the muster stations &amp; assembled. Warning of the passengers. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Seeing that they are suitably clad &amp; have donned their lifejackets correctly. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Keeping order in the passageways &amp; on the stairways &amp; generally controlling the movements of the passengers.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Ensuring that a supply of blankets is taken to the survival craft. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Muster lists prepared in the approved format should be prepared before the ship proceeds to sea &amp; updated whenever any changes take place. Passengers should be assisted as required during the drills &amp; other safety related routines.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level2 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.6.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Crew duties ? launching survival craft.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The lifeboats ? crew will do the following to prepare the lifeboats/ life-rafts and bring them to the embarkation deck: </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Remove the guardrail. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Two persons will go inside the lifeboat and remove the boat cover etc. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]-->Ship boat plugs (some boats have two plugs one forward and one aft and some boats have only one plug). </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Pass the toggle painter forward from inner side of the sling.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">5.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Check lifelines and falls clear. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">6.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Ship the rudder. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">7.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Check all lifeboat equipment to ensure that they are ready for use. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">8.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Test the lifeboat engine ahead and astern for a period of not less than 3 minutes. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">9.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Report to the bridge and, if permission given, let go the gripes and lower the boat to the embarkation deck.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Lifeboat under oars: When the boat is handled under oars, the coxswain of the boat is seated in the stern and the crew is seated on the thwarts facing aft. The crew of the boat should give proper strokes and pull with their back. For this it is imperative that the movement of oarsman should be free and without any obstruction. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The crew must pull together and keep good timing. With good practice, the crew should be able to pull together as the teamwork is essential for boat pulling Preparing the lifeboats and life-rafts for heavy weather: All the lifeboats and life-rafts must be made as sea worthy as possible before the onset of bad weather paying particular attention to the following points: Make sure that all lifeboats must be secured properly for sea, making sure that the keel of every boat must be resting on the davit chocks. The gripes must be properly tightened by means of its bottle screw and the ropes provided between the senhouse slips. Both the wire falls of the lifeboat must be properly checked and kept tight and should be clear of all lead blocks. No extra lashing should be put on the lifeboats, life-rafts or any life-saving appliance. Both the lifelines should be clear. Toggle painter should be made fast in the lifeboat and kept coiled on top of the No.1 thwart. Harbour safety pin should not be put when the ship proceeds to sea. All life-rafts and other buoyant apparatus should be checked for freedom of jettisoning. All float-free life-rafts system should not be put with any extra lashing except for the securing brands, which are designed to release when the hydrostatic release is activated and release the lashing in their cradles. If any extra lashing is put, it is wrong and could restrict even when the manual launching of the raft. Before the ship proceeds to sea, take a look at your rafts if they are secured by a painter to cradle or ship or week ? link with H.R.U.? These boats are fitted on with internal buoyancy tanks or compartments sufficient to float if the boat is flooded and open to the sea.</span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image.png\">\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level2 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.7.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Master ? ? s orders to abandon ship.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The order to abandon ship comes from the Master verbally.</span>\n</p>',NULL,'text',NULL,32767,0,'2026-04-19 03:45:28',1),(386,151,'4.	PERSONAL LIFE-SAVING APPLIANCES','<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level2 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Lifebuoys.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>An approved lifebuoy shall be capable of floating in fresh water at least 24 hours with ? kgs of iron suspended form it and must be constructed of solid cork, synthetic material or other equivalent material. Its inner diameter is 400 mm or 16 inches and the external diameter is 32 inches or 800 mm, its major axis shall be 6 inches or 15 cm and the minor axis shall be 4 inches or 10cm. With 4 equal distance bucketed to 9.5 mm of buoyant rope must be seized round the outside edge.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Distribution of lifebuoys on a ship: </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Lifebuoys must be readily available on both sides of the ship and at least one near than stern of the ship. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>One on each side of the ship and at mid-ship point - must have a buoyant line of length equal to twice the dropping height or 30 meters whichever is greater &amp; of 8 mm diameter, lifebuoys must be fitted with light markers functioning for 2 hours, flashing or continuous. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Two lifebuoys shall be provided with self-activating light and orange smoke signal and be capable of quick release from the navigating bridge in case any person falls overboard, burning period of light is 2 hours and for smoke 15 minutes. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Each lifebuoy shall be marked in block capital letters, the name of the ship and port of registry on which it is carried. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Have a mass of not less than 2.5 kg and not more than 6 kg.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Lifebuoy attached with light and smoke marker should not be less than 4 kg of weight.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Be fitted with 4 grab lines not less than 9.5 mm in diameter, 4 times the outer diameter and the grab line shall be secured at 4 equidistant points around the outer circumference of the lifebuoy to form four equal loops not less than 60cm each.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Light should not less than 2 candles power all round or 50 flashes per minute.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Buoyant line 30 m lengths, non-sinking, 9mm diameter and breaking strength of not less than one Kn. force.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Can be dropped from a stowage height of 30 meters and it should retain its shape. - Be of a highly visible orange color.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>It should not be adversely affected by oil or any oil products.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>A lifebuoy should not be painted in any other color except these required for ceremonial use.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>A lifebuoy should be of circular shape and is made of:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 111.45pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Polyurenthene or synthetic material.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 111.45pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Cork.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 111.45pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Kopak or balsa wood. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 111.45pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Thermacol, in cased or covered in plastic cover etc.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>How to get into a lifebuoy: The simplest way to get into a lifebuoy in the water is as follows: </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo5;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The man in the water should swim to the closest lifebuoy and place both of his hands on the topside nearest to you. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo5;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Your weight will then force the buoy down and lift the farther side, thus enabling you to insert your head and shoulders. Then the wearer should rest his arm on the sides of the lifebuoy. You will find yourself supported with your head and shoulder clear of the water till such time the help comes to you.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level2 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Lifejackets.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Total number of lifejackets provided on a ship is such that: </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>A lifejacket is provided for every adult person onboard the ship and 10% of adults ? number for children.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>A sufficient number for persons on watch &amp; for use at remotely located survival craft stations. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The lifejacket buoyancy may be achieved by.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Packing with buoyant material.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Inflating.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo6;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%281%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>A lifejacket</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Must have not less than 2 separate compartments.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>It shall inflate automatically on immersion or be capable of being inflated by mouth.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>It shall be marked with its serial number, its trademarks: M.O.T or D.O.T. stamp and the word FRONT are to be printed on front side of the lifejacket. A child lifejacket, which supports up to 32 kgs of weight, is marked as child, and a lifejacket which supports the weight 32 kg and more is marked adult. 5% extra lifejackets are carried on passenger ships and cargo ships.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Lifejackets should be stored on deck near embarkation deck or at muster stations. Every ship must carry an approved type of lifejacket for every person on board. Unless these can be adapted by children, a ship must also carry a sufficient number of lifejackets which are suitable for children. Lifejackets be fitted with a retro-reflective tapes or similar material. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%282%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Note: it is important to understand that lifejackets are issued to every individual onboard the ship, which support him in water until rescue comes.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The equipment ? s with lifejackets is:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Fixed or flashing light.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Whistle firmly secured by a cord.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>How to jump into the water with lifejacket on: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>As far as possible, avoid jumping into the water. Try to board a survival craft without getting wet or getting into the water; using a ladder, ropes, nets, lifeline, water hoses etc. Lifeboats and life-rafts which are launched by davits are boarded from the embarkation deck. If due to any reason, it becomes necessary to jump, you should do so from a height of not more than 4.5 m and make sure that the tapes/ straps of the lifejacket are tight. You must jump well clear of propellers. Before jumping makes sure that there are no obstructions in your way and that you are not jumping on to a boat or on the canopy of a lifeboat, life-raft or any such float. See around if any lifeboat or life-raft is nearer to you. Draw their attention by shouting or using whistle, then looking parallel to the horizon, hold down your lifejacket by one hand and block your nose with other, keep your feet together and never jump head first or dive, jump feet first, and swim clear of the ship or swim to the nearest lifeboat or life-raft in water &amp; board it. Do not try to swim for long distances. If lifeboat or life-raft is not seen around, you must swim well clear (about 100m away) of the vessel; you will be picked up by the rescue boat or other survival crafts later on.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Note: it is important to understand that lifejackets are issued to every individual onboard the ship, which support him in water until rescue comes.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The equipment ? s with lifejackets is:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Fixed or flashing light.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Whistle firmly secured by a cord.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>How to jump into the water with lifejacket on: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>As far as possible, avoid jumping into the water. Try to board a survival craft without getting wet or getting into the water; using a ladder, ropes, nets, lifeline, water hoses etc. Lifeboats and life-rafts which are launched by davits are boarded from the embarkation deck. If due to any reason, it becomes necessary to jump, you should do so from a height of not more than 4.5 m and make sure that the tapes/ straps of the lifejacket are tight. You must jump well clear of propellers. Before jumping makes sure that there are no obstructions in your way and that you are not jumping on to a boat or on the canopy of a lifeboat, life-raft or any such float. See around if any lifeboat or life-raft is nearer to you. Draw their attention by shouting or using whistle, then looking parallel to the horizon, hold down your lifejacket by one hand and block your nose with other, keep your feet together and never jump head first or dive, jump feet first, and swim clear of the ship or swim to the nearest lifeboat or life-raft in water &amp; board it. Do not try to swim for long distances. If lifeboat or life-raft is not seen around, you must swim well clear (about 100m away) of the vessel; you will be picked up by the rescue boat or other survival crafts later on.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%283%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level2 lfo1;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Immersion suits/anti-exposure suit.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>An immersion suit is constructed of waterproof rubber material such that: It can be unpacked and donned without any assistance within 2 minutes taking into account any associated clothing and lifejacket (if the immersion suit is to be worn in conjunction with a lifejacket). It should not sustain burning or continue melting after being totally enveloped in a fire for a period of 2 seconds. It covers the whole body with exception of the face; hands are also covered unless permanently attached gloves are provided. It is provided with arrangements to minimize or reduce free air in the legs of the suit.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>An immersion suit, which also complies with the requirements for a lifejacket, may be classified as a lifejacket also. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>An immersion suit shall permit the person wearing it and a lifejacket (if the immersion suit is to be worn with a lifejacket) to: climb up and down a vertical ladder at least 5m (16.25ft) in length, perform normal duties during abandonment, jump from a height of not less than 4.5 (14.9 ft) into the water without damaging or dislodging the immersion suit or being injured. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>An immersion suit, which has buoyancy and is designed to be worn without lifejacket, shall be fitted with a light and a whistle.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">e.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>If the immersion suit is to be worn in conjunction with a lifejacket, the lifejacket shall be worn over the immersion suit. A person wearing such an immersion suit shall be able to don lifejacket without assistance. An immersion suit should be available to every person assigned to the rescue boat.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>At least three immersion suits should be carried on each lifeboat for passenger and cargo ships with non-enclosed lifeboats.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%284%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level2 lfo1;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Thermal protective aids.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>A Thermal Protective Aid (TPA) is an aluminized polyethylene suit with heat sealed seams that protects from the elements and prevents hypothermia.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>It is defined by the International Life-Saving Appliance (LSA) Code as follows:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>A thermal protective aid shall be made of a waterproof material having a thermal conductance of not more than 7,800 W/(m ? K) and shall be so constructed that, when used to enclose a person, it shall reduce both the convective and evaporative heat loss from the wearer\'s body.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The thermal protective aid shall:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Cover the whole body of persons of all sizes wearing a lifejacket with the exception of the face. Hands shall also be covered unless permanently attached gloves are provided; </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Be capable of being unpacked and easily donned without assistance in a survival craft or rescue boat; and </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Permit the wearer to remove it in the water in not more than 2 min, if it impairs ability to swim.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The thermal protective aid shall function properly throughout an air temperature range -30 ? C to +20 ? C.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Every survival craft should have 2 thermal protective aid suits or 10% of its total carrying capacity whichever is greater. For the purpose of high visibility in all weather conditions the suit is colored in international orange.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Some manufacturers currently construct suits with discrete size bands (XS ? XXL) and others of a single ? universal ? size. Due to its usage conditions, it should be suitable to be worn over bulky clothing and lifejacket.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The following are the SOLAS Regulations for Immersion Suits; to know how many immersion suits are present onboard a ship, one should refer to the ? fire control and safety plan ? :</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Each person on board the ship must have his/her own personal immersion suit. Also, extra immersion suits should be provided for the watch keepers.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Immersion Suits may be of the kinds that are Insulated, Un-insulated, wearable with life jacket (all should have sufficient buoyancy).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Made of waterproof material.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Internationally RED in color which is highly visible. Note that most other LSA equipment is bright ORANGE.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">e.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The immersion suit should be unpacked and donned within a time frame of 2 minutes without any external help or assistance.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">f.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The individual must be able to jump from a height of at least 4.5 meters into water without injury to life of any damage to the immersion suit.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">g.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The suit should be able to cover the whole body except the face. Hands should be covered unless permanently attached gloves are provided.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">h.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Retro-reflective tapes must be fitted.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">i.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>It shall not sustain burning or continue to melt after being totally enveloped in a fire for a period of 2 seconds.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">j.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Normal work must be able to be carried out upon wearing.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">k.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The wearer should be capable of climbing up and down a vertical ladder of at least 5 meters in length.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">l.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The wearer must be able to swim a short distance.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">m.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The immersion suit is worn in cold weather when the temperature is below the freezing point.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">n.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The suit does not allow the body temperature to drop by more than 2 degrees when immersed for a period of 6 hours when the water temperature is between 0 and 2 degrees.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">o.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The wearer of the suit, with or without the lifejacket shall be able to turn from a face down position to a face-up position in not more than 5 second.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">p.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>If a lifejacket is required along with the immersion suit, then it should be worn over the immersion suit and without assistance.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo4;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%285%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Personal survival kits and group survival kits.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Lifeboat survival kits are stowed in inflatable or rigid ? lifeboats ? or ? life rafts. These kits provide basic survival tools and supplies to enable passengers to survive until they are rescued. </span><span>A lifeboat survival kit for 6 people can include: </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Water (9 litres).</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Emergency rations (3 ? kg).</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>First aid kit.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>36 anti-seasickness tablets.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>1 bailer.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>2 sponges.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>6 sickness bags.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>2 thermal survival blankets.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>1 ? </span><a title=\"Bellows\" href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bellows\"><span>bellows</span></a></span><span>/pump.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>3 leak stoppers.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>2 bottles of sunscreen.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>6 drinking cup.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>1 fishing kit.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>1 sea anchor and line.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>1 scissors.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>1 repair kit.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>2 parachute rocket.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>3 ? 6 hand flares.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>2 flashlights with extra batteries and bulbs.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>1 whistle.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>1 signaling mirror.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>1 smoke signal.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Survival instructions.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Table of life-saving signals.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%286%29.png\"></span>\n</p>',NULL,'text',NULL,32767,0,'2026-04-19 03:45:28',1),(387,151,'5.	Survival craft and rescue boats','<p style=\"margin-left: 14.2pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -14.2pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level2 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">5.1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Lifeboats.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Different types of lifeboats are:</span>\n</p>\n\n<ol style=\"margin-top: 0cm;\" start=\"1\" type=\"a\">\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span>Open lifeboats. </span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span>Partially enclosed lifeboats.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span>Self-righting partially enclosed lifeboats.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span>Totally enclosed lifeboats.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span>Self-righting totally enclosed lifeboats.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span>Totally enclosed lifeboats with a self-contained air support system.</span>\n</li>\n\n<li style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l2 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span>Fire-protected lifeboats. </span>\n</li>\n\n</ol>\n\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Lifeboats may be propelled by oars, sail, and mechanical power or by a compression ignition engine.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Capacity of lifeboats on passenger ships is generally sufficient for every person onboard. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Capacity of lifeboats on cargo ships is generally twice the number of persons onboard.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Brief description of different type of lifeboats: The criteria of construction and stability of lifeboats are governed by SOLAS Code. All lifeboats shall be of sufficient strength to enable them to be safety lowered into the water when loaded with their full complement of persons and equipment and be capable of being launched and towed when the ship is making headway at speed of 5 knots in calm water. Its hull and rigid canopy shall be fire retardant or non-combustible. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Seating shall be provided on the thwart, benches or fixed chairs fitted as low as practicable in the lifeboat. Each lifeboat shall be of sufficient strength to withstand, when applicable skates or fenders are in position, a lateral impact against the ship side at an impact velocity of at least 3.5 m/s and also a drop into the water from a height if at least 3 meters. The vertical distance between the floor surface and interior of the boat shall not be less than 1.3m for a lifeboat permitted to accommodate 21 persons or less and 1.7m for 24 persons and more. No lifeboat shall be approved to accommodate more than 150 persons having an average mass of 75 kg and all wearing lifejackets that can be seated in a normal position without interfering with the means of propulsion or the operation of any of the lifeboat ? s equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Open Lifeboats: The most commonly found lifeboat on cargo ships. There is no fixed canopy thus does not provide any protection from the elements of nature. Will have sail, oars, flemming gear or diesel-powered engine for propulsion.</span>\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Partially Enclosed lifeboat: These lifeboats are provided by a fixed canopy for protecting the survivors over some part of the lifeboat. Generally provided with oars and engines for propulsion.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Self-Righting Partially Enclosed lifeboat: When a partially enclosed lifeboat is provided by means of attaining positive stability even if launched incorrectly, such lifeboats are called ? Self Righting ? .</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Totally Enclosed Lifeboat: These boats are provided by a fixed canopy completely covering the upper part of the boat and thus forming a capsule to protect the survivors. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Self-Righting totally enclosed lifeboat: Totally enclosed lifeboat having means of positive stability. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Totally enclosed lifeboat with self-contained air support system: Provided with self-contained air support system for use in case of fire. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Fire Protected: Provided with sprinklers outside the hull of the boat to keep it cool in case of fire in vicinity of launch area.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Launching Appliances: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Lifeboats are launched either by davits or by free fall method. Safety precautions are to be taken for personal safety while launching lifeboats, as serious casualties/ injuries can take place. Embarkation into the survival crafts can be at the embarkation decks or by embarkation ladder or even from the water after jumping over side.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Launching of open type lifeboats: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The harbour safety pins are removed, ensure shipside is clear.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Check for the boat plugs in place.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The rudder is shipped.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The painter is passed forward and secured to a strong point.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">5.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The lifelines are clear, the bowman and stern sheet are standing firmly and holding the lifelines.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">6.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The boat is then lowered to the embarkation deck lifting the brake.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">7.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The bowsing-in tackles are made fast, after which the tricing pendants are removed; the crew and passengers now embark the lifeboat. The bowsing-in tackles are slacked easily till there is no weight on them. Lifeboat engine started.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">8.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The tackles are then let go and the brakes-man/winch-man lowers boat down to the water level.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">9.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The forward and aft hooks are unhooked- preferably simultaneously. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo2;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">10.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The winch-man now boards the boat by the embarkation ladder. The forward most toggle painter is cast off, keeping the tiller of the lifeboat towards the vessel to take the head of the boat away form the vessel and move away from the ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Launching of totally enclosed lifeboats: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Remove harbour safety pins and ensure that it is all clear over side and safe to lower the boat. Confirm painter is secured to bitts on deck or other strong point, Open the entry door and embark the passengers. If the gripes are not the automatic release type, they should be released now and cleared away from the boat. Everybody on board the lifeboat should be seated properly and with safety belts secured. If there is possibility of toxic vapours around the vessel, close the natural vents on the boat and start the air support system. The engine is started, the tiller is kept towards the vessel, the remote lowering control wire is pulled and boat is again lowered. When the boat is in the water, the hook release mechanism is operated and the hooks disconnected, and boat shoved-off if required. If necessary, the water spray system is engaged and the water spray started to protect the boat against any surrounding fire. The sea anchor is used to keep the boats head into the wind and the sea. It is streamed from the bows.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Launching of totally enclosed Free-fall lifeboats: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>In addition to all precautions taken prior to launch of a lifeboat there are some special considerations to be given to Free Fall launched lifeboats.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Check that the launch area is clear of obstructions to some distance in the direction of launch.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Check the launching ramp is clear of obstructions.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>There should be sufficient depth available for launch.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>All passengers and crew must be seated and secured properly by safety harness provided with the seat.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>No lines should be tied on board.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Precautions while launching: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Most of the precautions are already listed in the launching procedure. Additionally following precautions need to be observed: </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Crew should hold on to lifeline while launching the lifeboat.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Be very careful of the floating hooks of the lifeboat falls. A lot of injuries to fingers and heads have been reported during launching and recovery of lifeboat.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Release mechanism should be carefully checked prior to launch. On-load release mechanism is a very common cause of accidents.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Embarkation </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>All efforts should be made to keep oneself dry as far as practicable, while embarking the survival craft. Lifeboat davits are capable of launching survival craft with full complement and equipment. Thus if possible embark the lifeboat at embarkation deck. If time and conditions allow, embark into the survival craft by climbing down on the embarkation ladder. Only as a very last resort, jump into water. Crew already in the survival craft should be alert towards others who may be in the water. All efforts should be made to recover those in the water.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 14.2pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -14.2pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level2 lfo5;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">5.2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Life-rafts.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: -.05pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Main types of life-rafts:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Inflatable life-rafts. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--> <span>Rigid life-rafts.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.95pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l3 level1 lfo6;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%281%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Float-free arrangements for life-rafts - Hydrostatic release unit: The HRU is an extension of the securing lashings over the inflatable life-raft. The lashing itself must incorporate a senhouse slip to allow manual release. If the vessel suffers a disaster and subsequently sinks the release unit will automatically activate under the surface of water. Operation of this unit takes place up to the depth of 4m where the pressure is sufficient to release the draw bolt.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%282%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>RELEASE OF LIFERAFT FITTED WITH HRU </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Once the draw bolt is released the web straps no longer retain the raft canister in the cradle, the life-raft is free to float clear of the surface. The painter line is extracted as the raft rises clear of the sinking vessel. The painter is secured to the D ring, which was previously retained by the draw bolt. The arrangement provides a weak link, which parts after the painter becomes fully extended &amp; the pressure increase cause the safe working load/ breaking strain to be exceeded. Inflation can be expected to take place on the surface only when the painter is fully extended &amp; the canister starts to snatch over the painter length.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%283%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 14.2pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -14.2pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level2 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">5.3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Rescue boats.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n</a><span>Minimum number of rescue boats on a passenger ship ? Passenger ships shall carry one or two rescue boats (one on each side) if less than or more than 500 GT respectively; a lifeboat may be accepted as a rescue boat provided that it also complies with the requirements for a rescue boat.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk113050340;\"><span>Minimum number of rescue boats on a cargo ship ? Cargo ships shall carry at least one rescue boat; a lifeboat may be accepted as a rescue boat provided that it also complies with the requirements for a rescue boat.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk113050340;\"><span>A rescue boat construction is also the same as general requirements of lifeboats. Rescue boat may be either of rigid or inflated construction or a combination of both. They should not be less than 8.5 m in length and capable of carrying capacity the boat certified for. Capable of manoeuvring at speed of 6 knots and can tow a 25 persons capacity loaded life raft at a speed of 6 knots and can be righted up by 2 persons in case capsized. It should be able to be retrieved from the water. It is used for marshal life rafts and be fitted with a bow cover extending over not less than 15% of the boat ? s lengths be equipped with thwarts, benches or fixed chairs for everyone that the boat is designed to accommodate. A rescue boat shall be fitted either with an outboard motor or an inboard engine, with rudder and tiller.</span>\n</p>',NULL,'text',NULL,32767,0,'2026-04-19 03:45:28',1),(388,151,'6. Ship abandonment and survival','<p style=\"margin-left: 54.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level2 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Actions to be taken when required to abandon ship.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 54.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level2 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Actions to be taken when in the water.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n</a><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>DANGERS TO SURVIVORS: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk113050549;\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Heat &amp; Sun stroke:</span></strong></span><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk113050549;\"><span> A serious condition where the body temperature rises above the normal to 40 deg C or more. It is caused by prolonged exposure to very hot or very humid condition. The symptoms are hot dry skin with a rapid pulse rate. The patient may outwardly appear flushed &amp; could experience some confusion. Unconsciousness may follow. Treatment - to treat heat stroke, remove the patient ? s clothing &amp; endeavour to keep the body cool. Wrap in a cold sheet &amp; sponge the body with cold water. Maintain cooling the body by fanning &amp; sponging, when necessary, until the temperature starts to fall. Place the casualty in recovery position &amp; cover with a dry cloth or sheet.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk113050549;\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Effects of sea sickness:</span></strong></span><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk113050549;\"><span> The biggest problem while experiencing sea sickness is the nausea and vomiting. This causes dehydration and general feeling of depression. Inside the survival craft it is imperative that appropriate sea sickness medicine is taken by each and every individual ? even seafarers with long experience can get sea sick on survival craft.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk113050549;\"><span>Failure to maintain body fluids (dehydration)</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Drinking sea water: Must be avoided at all costs. It will add to the feeling of thirst and can cause severe problems for the kidneys. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Fire or oil on water: Should there be oil or water on the water, while the crew is evacuating there is another element of danger involved that can causeinjury. If one has to swim in these conditions it will be very difficult as it will be almost impossible to surface for breathing. If one is not inside a totally enclosed fire protected lifeboat with air support system, use of wet towels to cover the face and exposed parts of the body is recommended, when in the lifeboat.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk113050549;\"><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Sharks:</span></strong></span><span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk113050549;\"><span> In shark infested areas, the people should group together (knot lash each other facing outwards). Retain all clothing especially on legs and feet. Keep quiet and as stationary as possible and only move to keep the shark in sight.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk113050549;\"><span>Rescuing persons from the sea:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Even if someone has boarded a survival craft after abandonment, he won ? t be a survivor until he has been rescued. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Rescue of survivors may be by small boats or the ship itself. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Survivors in the water or aircraft should usually be rescued first and those safe in rafts last.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The main dangers which a person in the water are exposed to are drowning and hypothermia. Person in the water should avoid unnecessary swimming. If there is more than one person, they should group together. </span>\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>When rescuing the persons from the sea, if the capacity of the craft does not allow pick-up of all persons in the water in one time, at least the moral of the persons in the water must be kept high or pulled-up.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk113050549;\"><span>For survivors in the water, the rescuing vessel may find it necessary to: </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Rig scrambles nets.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Launch lifeboats/ life-rafts.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Have crew members suitably equipped to enter the water to assist survivors</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Be prepared to provide initial medical treatment.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span style=\"mso-bookmark: _Hlk113050549;\"><span>For a fire or extremely heavy weather, or where it is impossible for the rescue ship to come alongside, and then a lifeboat or life-raft may be towed to a closer position.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 54.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -36.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level2 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Actions to be taken when aboard a survival craft.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Clearing away from ship ? After launching into the water, the survival crafts should be steamed away (or rowed away) clear away from the ship in order to avoid getting sucked in the low pressure (vacuum) created by the sinking ship. But also, survival crafts should not be steamed very far away as the immediate rescue will more often come to the position at which the ship transmitted the emergency (distress) signals.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Other factors to take care of are:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Prudent use of fresh water &amp; food. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Need to avoid dehydration. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Measures for survival in case of fire or oil on water. - Means of survival in shark infested waters. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Correct use of sea anchors / drogues. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Duties of a look out.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Means of facilitating detection by others. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Means of maintaining morals. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Use &amp; working of shark repellents.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Means of maintaining morale </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>When survivors are in the survival craft the in charge should raise the morale of the persons who are in the survival craft by keeping the people and their mind busy by a good routine and discipline maintained at the outset of the situation. A watch system should be brought into operation. The survivors should communicate their location by using emergency transmitter. This should be activated as soon as practicable. The lookout should be aware of the position of pyrotechnics and how to use instructions being read well before they actually need to be used. Getting people to read the instructions on equipment and designating various job functions are the ways in which the hopes of the survivors are kept alive. Morale is maintained and will to survive is not lost. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The well-being of survivors is important and should be given priority. After having decided the direction that the survival craft should take, the other important survival factors should be borne in mind. The crews ? and passengers ? morale should be kept high. A positive action is always a good morale booster. The in-charge should try to win the confidence of the survivors in the boat. The survivors should be kept busy, as much as possible and they should therefore be allotted with watches and other duties. The crew should be made responsible for bailing out water from the boat regularly, collecting rainwater and tending to sea anchor. The duties should be allotted to look after the injured. Even for the smallest jobs, duties must be allotted, like looking after the equipment ? s and valuables. A reliable man should be deputed to issue the rations. The rations provided in the survival craft are based upon the study of the survival conditions. Read instructions from the survival handbook supplied in the raft. Do not issue food or water for the first 24 hours. The body is already full of water if more water is put in it will be wasted in the form of urine.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>After 24 hours, the body is dried and will absorb any water that is drunk. Only if a man is injured or unconscious then he may be given water in the first 24 hours to replace loss of fluid due to bleeding or burns. After 24 hours, preferably, only two ounces of water at a time may be issued, but do not issue more than half a litter per person per day. But also, do not go below this ration. In hot climate, water should be issued preferably after sunset. The rations should be issued to all survivors at the same time. After 24 hours, issue a full ration, three times daily at sunrise, midday and sunset. Do not be tempted to give more rations than necessary. Make sure that carbohydrate food is taken. The lifeboat and life-raft rations are non-thirst provoking. Eat fish only if plenty supply of water is available.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>A first aid kit is provided in the equipment and may be used, if required. Do not waste your pyrotechnics unnecessarily in order to attract the attention. The fuel should be conserved as much as possible and made use only in emergency It is imperative to take seasickness pills as early as possible. The pills themselves will make mouth feel dry, but resist the urge to drink water No one should go out swimming. Keep the canopy of the boat wet so that temperature inside the life raft can be made low Alcohol should never be taken and never drink urine.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Lookout </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>A watch system should be organized, giving some duty to each occupant of a survival craft. Having something to do or to be responsible as it improves the morale of individual survivors and of the group in general. A constant lookout should be maintained to keep watch all-round the horizon. The lookout must be instructed to listen as well as watch. They should be alert for signs of ships, aircraft, land, dangers and rain showers in the vicinity, which may provide an opportunity to supplement the water rate. On making any sighting the lookout should inform the person in charge of the craft before doing anything.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Means of survival if in water and not in lifeboat or life-raft (Survival in water) </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Any person involved in an emergency should strive to remain, alive. It is known that the body loses its heat twenty-six times higher when immersed hi water than when kept in a dry environment. For this reason, a waterproof suit must be considered extremely essential for survival However, for persons finding themselves in water, the following actions are recommended:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Avoid unnecessary swimming. This will only use up valuable energy and increase the body ? s heat loss to surrounding water. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Float on the surface in a HELP (Heat Escape Lessening Posture) position with the knees clasped up against the chest. This position conserves body heat. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Secure the face visor of your lifejacket if attached. Failing this, protect your airways against breaking seas by using your hands to form a face bar. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>If there is more than one person, group together. There is safety in number as you are making a larger target when grouped on the surface. If survivor ? s forma Circle group, free flowing water is restricted in the center of the circle and the loss of heat is reduced. Casualties can also be kept warmer by being placed in the middle of the circle and supported by the others. Lifelines fitted to certain suits can also be employed to link survivors together.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">e.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Keep your morale up. Keep the will to survive. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo6;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">f.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--> <span>Maintain as much of your body out of the water as possible throughout and avoid panic actions.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo6;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image.png\"></span>\n</p>',NULL,'text',NULL,32767,0,'2026-04-19 03:45:28',1),(389,151,'7. Emergency radio equipment','<p style=\"margin-left: 18.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level2 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">7.1<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Two-way VHF.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<span>If the distress call is received no rescue possible. ? Many lives were lost due to lack of effective means. ? Formerly everything depended on the lookout and closeness between castaway and help. ? It took centuries until a technological advancement, radio, multiplied the scope of the call, the number of receivers and the speed of response. ? An undoubted progress based on relief assistance of the nearest ships heard the call for help, which is hardly the case in areas of poor navigation. ? The special language used, the uncertainty in the reception and communication congestion in some areas accounted correctable limitations in current times. ? Digital technology and satellites provide global reach and reliability and add to existing means for forming a valuable rescue communication system: the Global System for Maritime Distress and Safety (GMDSS).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<span>The procedures and rules described below are mandatory in the Maritime Mobile Service and are designed to enable the exchange of messages between stations and allowing efficient receiving a message of danger.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<span>The frequencies for calls and distress traffic on VHF telephony are 156.8 MHz (Channel 16) and on medium wave 2182 kHz. ? In digital selective calling (DSC) are VHF 156.525 MHz (Channel 70) and 2187.5 Khz medium wave.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<strong><span>LIMITATIONS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<span>The duration of the communication channels for relief, except in danger, be limited to the minimum essential to establish contact and agree on the working channel, and must not exceed one minute. ? Are prohibited any emission capable of causing harmful interference to distress, alarm, urgency or safety, in particular:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Transmissions in ? Useful Features</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The transmission ? or false signals or dare cheating</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Transmissions are ? superfluous signals and correspondence</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The transmission ? is without IDENTIFICATION</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><span>HAZARD MESSAGE, URGENT AND SAFETY</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Three levels of distress messages depending on the severity of the situation: danger (MAYDAY), urgency (PAN-PAN) and security (SECURITE).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<strong><span>Danger</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<span>\"MAYDAY\" repeated three times (MAYDAY, MAYDAY, MAYDAY) and pronounced (ESM ESM ESM) serves to warn of serious and imminent danger.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<strong><span>Be used always obeying the following rules</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Should be used or what if you need immediate assistance.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>For emission ? or n frequencies used 2182 ? kHz ? or VHF channel 16</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Speak clearly and slowly, pronouncing the number and letters at a time.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>If there are language problems, you should use the International Code of Signals.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The coastal resorts of Maritime Telephone Service and Salvage Centers are listening 24 hours a day, every day of the year.However, if we hear from a boat a distress call and that there is no answer to their distress call, we will use our radio to make a distress call to potential original recipients and head to the area of the call.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<strong><span>Message content</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Vessel Name</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Situation ? (coordinates or bearing and distance)</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Reason for distress call</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>MAYDAY calls are rules that should be familiar to users of pleasure craft:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>SILENCE MAYDAY ? </span></strong><span>: to be with the vessel ( or receiving station) may impose silence on all vessels is to broadcast on that frequency.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>SILENCE FINI</span></strong><span>: indicates the end of silence.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>PRUDENCE</span></strong><span>: allows to resume traffic, but narrowly.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>MAYDAY REL ?œ </span></strong><span>:to be used by a station or who knows that a ship is in danger but cannot issue or needing help and cannot come to his aid, or has heard the acknowledgment a third station.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<strong><span>URGENT (PAN-PAN)</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<span>\"PAN-PAN\" repeated three times (PAN-PAN, PAN-PAN, PAN-PAN) is used to transmit urgent messages relevant to the safety of a vessel or of persons, although there is no serious or immediate danger. ? For example, if a fault occurs in our engine and we want to be given the proper help, we will issue a message of \"urgency\" (PAN-PAN) and not one of distress (MAYDAY). ? Takes priority over all communications except distress (MAYDAY).</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<strong><span>SECURITY (SECURITE)</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<span>\"SECURITE\" repeated three times (SECURITE, SECURITE, SECURITE) is used to transmit messages concerning the safety of navigation or important meteorological warnings.</span>\n</p>\n <table style=\"width: 444.75pt; border-collapse: collapse; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184;\" border=\"0\" width=\"890\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\"> <tbody> <tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; height: 24.75pt;\"> <td style=\"width: 444.75pt; border: solid black 1.0pt; background: white; padding: .75pt .75pt .75pt .75pt; height: 24.75pt;\" nowrap=\"nowrap\" width=\"890\"> \n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n</a><strong><span>Radiotelephone Procedure</span></strong>\n</p>\n </td> </tr> <tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1; mso-yfti-lastrow: yes; height: 59.25pt;\"> <td style=\"width: 444.75pt; border: solid black 1.0pt; border-top: none; mso-border-top-alt: solid black 1.0pt; padding: .75pt .75pt .75pt .75pt; height: 59.25pt;\" width=\"890\"> <table style=\"width: 441.75pt; border-collapse: collapse; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184;\" border=\"0\" width=\"884\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\"> <tbody> <tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; height: 15.0pt;\"> <td style=\"width: 177.0pt; padding: .75pt .75pt .75pt .75pt; height: 15.0pt;\" nowrap=\"nowrap\" width=\"354\"> \n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; margin: 0cm 7.0pt .0001pt 0cm;\">\n</a><strong><span>Message</span></strong>\n</p>\n </td> <td style=\"width: 265.5pt; padding: .75pt .75pt .75pt .75pt; height: 15.0pt;\" width=\"531\"> \n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; margin: 0cm 7.0pt .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><span>Meaning</span></strong>\n</p>\n </td> </tr> <tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1;\"> <td style=\"width: 177.0pt; padding: .75pt .75pt .75pt .75pt;\" width=\"354\"> \n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; margin: 0cm 7.0pt .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><span>MAYDAY, MAYDAY, MAYDAY</span></strong>\n</p>\n </td> <td style=\"width: 265.5pt; padding: .75pt .75pt .75pt .75pt;\" width=\"531\"> \n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; margin: 0cm 7.0pt .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><span>Indicates that a ship, aircraft and other transportation are threatened by grave and imminent danger and requests immediate assistance.</span></strong>\n</p>\n </td> </tr> <tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 2;\"> <td style=\"width: 177.0pt; padding: .75pt .75pt .75pt .75pt;\" width=\"354\"> \n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; margin: 0cm 7.0pt .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><span>PAN-PAN, PAN-PAN, PAN-PAN</span></strong>\n</p>\n </td> <td style=\"width: 265.5pt; padding: .75pt .75pt .75pt .75pt;\" width=\"531\"> \n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; margin: 0cm 7.0pt .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><span>Indicates that the calling station has a very urgent message to transmit related to the security of a ship, aircraft and other means of transport by water, or safety of a person.</span></strong>\n</p>\n </td> </tr> <tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 3; mso-yfti-lastrow: yes;\"> <td style=\"width: 177.0pt; padding: .75pt .75pt .75pt .75pt;\" width=\"354\"> \n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; margin: 0cm 7.0pt .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><span>SECURITE, SECURITE, SECURITE</span></strong>\n</p>\n </td> <td style=\"width: 265.5pt; padding: .75pt .75pt .75pt .75pt;\" width=\"531\"> \n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; margin: 0cm 7.0pt .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><span>Indicates that the station will transmit a message concerning the safety of navigation or important meteorological warning.</span></strong>\n</p>\n </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> </td> </tr> </tbody> </table> \n\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l4 level2 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">7.2<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Emergency position-indicating radio beacons (EPIRBs).</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Under the GMDSS, one of the significant changes is that Distress Alerting in ship-to-shore direction has taken precedence. EPIRBs are used as a secondary means of distress alerting.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The basic purpose of an EPIRB is to help determine the position of survivors during search and rescue operations. The EPIRB signals indicate that one or more persons are in distress, they may no longer be on board a ship or aircraft and that receiving facilities may not be available. Depending upon trading pattern of the vessel, appropriate EPIRB has to be carried on board e.g. for vessels sailing in Sea Area A1 only, VHF EPIRB will suffice. COSPAS-SARSAT EPIRB can be used in any sea area but InmarsatE EPIRB is suitable for sea areas A1, A2 and A3 only. EPIRBs should be so located that they are easily accessible and have an unobstructed view of the sky. It is a mandatory requirement that all EPIRBs should have arrangements for manual activation or in the event of a ship sinking, of being capable of releasing automatically (HRU), floating free and self-activation.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>All types of EPIRBs are required to be equipped with a light of 0.75 candela, having a low duty-cycle flashing ratio, which is automatically activated by the onset of darkness. The battery must last for at least 48 hours. Lithium batteries are used because of their high reliability.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Inmarsat-E: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>These EPIRBs operate at 1.6 GHz (L-band) through the INMARSAT GEOSTATIONERY SATELLITES covering all ocean regions except the Polar Regions (North and South of Latitude 75 degrees approx). Distress alerts transmitted from Inmarsat-E Emergency Position Indicating Radio Beacons (EPIRBs) are relayed through Inmarsat satellites to dedicated receiving equipment located at four Coast Earth Stations (CESs): Raisting (Germany); Niles Canyon (USA); Perth (Australia); and BT Atlantic (UK). For each Ocean region, there are two dedicated RCCs e.g. RCC Bremen and RCC Canberra for the Indian Ocean Region; RCC Falmouth (U.K.) and RCC Bremen for the AOR (E).The distress alert transmitted by an EPIRB will always be received by two Coast Earth Stations in each ocean region, giving 100 percent duplication for each ocean region in case of failures or outages associated with any of the CESs. Following reception of the distress alert, it is immediately forwarded automatically to a Maritime Rescue Co-ordination Centre (MRCC) so that appropriate action can be taken.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>ALL EPIRBs have to be registered, giving details of the vessel or craft on which they are installed, as soon as possible after installation. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>MRCCs hold details of all registered Inmarsat-E, EPIRBs and as the position contained in the distress alert is accurate to within 200 meters, Search and Rescue (SAR) authorities can be alerted within minutes for appropriate action. The time taken from the transmission of a distress alert to reception at the MRCC is within five minutes and typically under two minutes.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>An Inmarsat-E EPIRB consists of a buoy, which carries an antenna and the necessary electronics, batteries, an interface to ships power supplies, a control panel and remote activator. Most EPIRBs have an integral GPS receiver enabling the position to be kept updated automatically at all times. Some EPIRBs incorporate a 9 GHz radar transponder for the purpose of providing a locating (homing) signal.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level2 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">7.3<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Search and rescue transponders (SART/AIS-SART).</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>',NULL,'text',NULL,32767,0,'2026-04-19 03:45:28',1),(390,151,'8. SURVIVOR´S ACTION WHEN BEING RESCUED','<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>. Communicating with the rescue ship and helicopter.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>COMMUNICATING WITH THE HELICOPTER: </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">A)<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? </span></span></strong><!--[endif]--><strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Hand &amp; Arms signal </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The officer in charge will be responsible for signaling to the helicopter. The following visual signals should be used ? Forward ? signal given to the helicopter pilot to indicate that the vessel is ready &amp; the helicopter may approach. (Arms repeatedly moved upward and backward, beckoning onward.)</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Finishing operations ? signal given to helicopter pilot to indicate operations finished. (Arms repeatedly crossed above the head.)</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%281%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Through shore station</span></strong><span> ? using radio equipment provided onboard ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>8.2. Survivor ? ? s preparation before being rescued.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>EVACUATION FROM SHIP &amp; SURVIVAL CRAFT</span></strong>\n</p>\n\nThe purpose of the SAR helicopter is to provide assistance to ships out at sea to pick up casualty or to carry out rescue operations. These can rescue 10 to 18 survivors depending on the type or capacity to carry out rescue operations. They do not normally operate more than 450 nautical miles both ways from its base, though some more distance may be increased depending on type of situation, and have VHF radio and perhaps 2182 KHz R/T fitted with. Before the helicopter takes off from its base, the helicopter pilot must be provided with some of the information, by the master of the vessel, such as name of the ship, its colour, course, speed and shop should fly colour pendant on its mast at day time and weather condition visibility a distance from the land. And night </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>area of the operation must be well illuminated. Operation of a helicopter depends on circumstances of the bad weather conditions and visibility. The master of the vessel in distress must have efficient communication between all parties is essential so that rescue operation can be carried out in an efficient manner to the distressed victims. The time factor also plays a large part in helicopters, with an effective range of 299 nautical miles one way approximately, the actual range is in fact considerably greater but allowance must be made for hovering over and above the scene of operation. On a ship, a pick-up space, which is clear of mast riggings &amp; other impediments, has to be used for winching or landing by helicopter. For evacuating from lifeboats &amp; life-rafts, only winching can be used.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>8.3. Actions to be taken by the survivor when being rescued by ship.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><span>Be listening on VHF channel 16</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%282%29.png\"></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Keep your nose to the wind direction.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%283%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Clear the deck.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%284%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Call the attention of the helicopter.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%285%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Let the hoisting wire ? tap water or the boat before touching you.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%286%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span>DO NOT TIE CABLE GUIDE EVER!</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%287%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span>DO NOT RELEASE THE CABLE.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3; margin: 5.0pt 0cm 5.0pt 0cm;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%288%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<span>Make sure the harness well ? before being hoisted.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 3;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%289%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong><span>RESCUE BOAT FROM SURVIVAL</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2810%29.png\"></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2811%29.png\"></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>8.4. Actions to be taken by the survivor when being rescued by helicopter.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -.1pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Precautions to be taken for helicopter evacuation: </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Do not touch the metal wire or the metal part of the strop before first touching it to any ship structure. This is necessary to discharge static charge build up. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Never attach the recovery line or wire to any part of the ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Recovery area should be free of obstructions and clear of any loose objects.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Injured personnel should be properly secured in the stretcher for a vertical lift.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 35.9pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">e.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>All available lighting should be made available in case of rescue operations in darkness.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>HELICOPTER PICK-UP: </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Methods of pick-up are by: </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Harness.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Stretcher.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Rescue net.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Hand &amp; arm signals for safe lifting: </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Embarking: place strop under armpits, stand vertically under the helicopter winch, when ready give the ? thumbs up ? sign, hold on to the strop with both hands with elbows tucked well in; at the helicopter doorway the winch man will turn you to face outward, and will then assist you into the helicopter, do not remove strop until instructed to do so, sit where the winch man directs you, fasten your seat belt &amp; study the in-flight safety regulations.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Disembarking: do not leave your seat until instructed, the winch man will check that the strop is properly fitted, sit in the doorway when the winch man instructs you to do so &amp; give the ? thumbs up ? sign when ready, when you have reached the deck let the strop fall to your feet &amp; step well clear of it, leave the operating area briskly, keeping head well down. It is very important that the instructions given by helicopter pilot must be obeyed.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>CORRECT USE OF HELICOPTER HARNESS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Additional information for helicopter operations:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2812%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The officer in-charge of the ship should make sure that before the helicopter approaches the ship, the WINCHING AREA must be seriously considered such as (1) on the open deck forward or aft (2) on the life boat (3) on the life-raft and necessary arrangement on the ship to assist winching operations in order to ensure that the helicopters may operate safely without risk to persons on board or in the helicopter itself should be made:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The winching area should, as far as practicable, be located on the port side of the ship, such that a large portion of the maneuvering zone extends over the ship side and situated where it will enable the pilot to have an obstructed view of the ship. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The area yellow in a circular manner and a letter H painted yellow in the centre of the circle. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The height of obstacles such as ventilators, small deck houses area companion ways should be removed which is more than 3 m height. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Side rails and where necessary, wings stanchions should be lower to deck level.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">5.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>All loose gear rags, cotton waste must be secured. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">6.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The operating area should be if possible free of heavy weather spray. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]-->\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Deck party should be ready and all passengers should be clear of the operating place.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Deck party should be aware that rescue operation is about to take place or taking place.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Fire pump should be running and adequate water pressure should be running in the fire hose of the operating area.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">5.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Portable foam type extinguishers should be ready. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">6.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>A rescue party, wearing fireman ? s outfits should be ready.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">7.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>A man-overboard rescue boat should be ready. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">8.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Portable fire extinguishers, large axe, wire cutters, torches etc., should be ready. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">9.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>In the case of tankers, inert gas system should be ready and the operating area should be vented to the atmosphere. 30 minutes before the operation is due to start.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">10.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Helicopter will normally be approaching the winching area along a flight path on the port side of the ship and a wind pendant is hoisted in a position where it can be readily seen by the pilot of the helicopter. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">11.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>If winching operation have to be conducted hours of darkness, then it will be necessary to illuminate the manoeuvring one the mustering area and the wind pendant and lighting should be not directed at the sea or toward the helicopter. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo1;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">12.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>All significant obstruction such as mast, funnel, derricks should be illuminated by flood lights.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Head the ship according to the pilot ? s recommendation. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>If the winching operation cannot be performed on the deck, then the survivors to be rescued, astern of the vessel in a raft on long painter about 100 to 120 meters or if the helicopter cannot pick up direct form the ship and if the life raft is not available then the survivors to be rescued in a open or totally enclosed lifeboat having top canopy hatch and railing all around to protect the people from falling overboard.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Never touch the winch wire until it is earthed with sea water or ship structure as it is carrying static electricity. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Never secure the winch wire on deck or allow it to get fouled.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Note: the pilot and crew of the helicopter are professionals in rescue operation to be carried out either form the ship ? s deck or from water. The method employed by this helicopter in winching operating is as follows. </span>\n</p>\n\nI.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Lower a crew member from the helicopter to the man if he is helpless. </span>\n</p>\n\nII.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Lowering a strop slip to a person on the winch wire (if he is able to help himself). </span>\n</p>\n\nIII.</span><!--[endif]--><span>Helicopter lifting strop should be used as under.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Take the strop and put both the head and the arms through the loop. And secure the strop under the armpit with the padded point positioned as high as possible across the back. Tighten up the strop by pulling down the toggle as low as possible near the chest. When securing the strop extend one arm and give the thumbs up signal to the when man in the helicopter. Rescue also can be performed by means of the following:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Rescue litter or stretcher.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Rescue net.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo3;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Rescue basket.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Evacuation of a casualty by a helicopter: </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Evacuation can be done by landing operation on vessels well suited for landing of helicopters, else by winching on the other vessels. Before a helicopter operation can be conducted, information must be exchanged between the ship &amp; the helicopter. Radio link by marine VHF must be established. Helicopter uses DF for homing the radio transmissions from a ship in ascertaining the ship ? s position.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Information to be exchanged &amp; precautions to take include the following:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Information of vessel:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Position of the vessel </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Course and speed to the rendezvous position.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Local weather conditions.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>How to identify the vessel from the air (such as flags, orange smoke signals, spotlights, daylight signaling lamps).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Clearly marked winching area with clear access to the operating area and exit from it to the shipside identified. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The operating area must be well lit.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">d.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>All the loose objects within and adjacent to the operating area must secured or removed.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">e.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>All aerials, standing or running gear above the operating area must be secured or removed. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">f.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>A pennant or windsock must be hoisted where it can be clearly seen by the helicopter pilot.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">g.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Communication established between the bridge and the deck officer by the use of walkie- talkie/ VHF. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">h.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Deck party is complete, correctly dressed and in position. Deck party must be wearing brightly colored waistcoats and protective helmets, and are all passengers clear of the operating area. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">i.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>A rescue party must be detailed and rescue boat (for personoverboard) ready for lowering.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">j.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Following equipment to be kept ready: large axe crowbar, wire cutters, red emergency signal/torch, marshalling batons, first-aid equipment. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">k.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Correct lighting (including special navigation lights) must be switched on prior to night operations and not directed towards the helicopter. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">l.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The hook handler must be equipped with helmet, strong rubber gloves and rubber-soled shoes to avoid the danger of static discharge.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">m.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Just before the helicopter arrives, radar and other equipment (which use electromagnetic waves) must be switched-off. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">n.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Fire-fighting equipment or its equivalent to be made ready:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Fire pumps running with adequate pressure in deck line, fire hoses rigged, connected and ready for use kept near to but clear of the operating area. The deck water system must be capable of delivering at least 2 jets of water to any part of the helicopter operating area.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>At least 2 fire hose nozzles which should be of the dual-purpose type.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>A suitable foam application system (fixed or portable), capable of delivering a foam solution at a rate of not less than 6 liters per minute for each square meter of clear zone and sufficient foam compound to enable the rate to be maintained for at least five minutes. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Fire hoses and foam nozzles must point away from the operating area in case of inadvertent discharge. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>At least 2 dry powder type fire extinguishers with an aggregate capacity of not less than 45kg.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Carbon di-oxide type fire extinguishers with an aggregate capacity of not less than 18kg. </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Fire resistant blankets and gloves.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Sufficient fire proximity suits.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Portable fire-fighting equipment for oil fires must be stationed near the disembarkation space.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">o.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>All crew members concerned, as well as the persons to be evacuated, should wear lifejackets. Loose clothing or headgear must be removed.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 36.0pt; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal; mso-list: l7 level1 lfo4;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">p.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The lifting device on the end of the winch cable must never secured to any part of the ship or must not be entangled in the rigging of fixtures. Also, the ship ? s personnel should not attempt to grasp the lifting device until requested/ allowed to do so by the helicopter crew. A metal part of the lifting device should first be allowed to touch the deck in order to avoid possible shock due to static electricity.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Additional checks/ precautions for landing on:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The deck party must be aware and ready.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The operating area free of heavy spray or seas on deck.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Side rails/ awnings/ stanchions and other obstructions must be lowered or removed, portable pipes removed and remaining apex ends blanked-off.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>If the helicopter pilot decides that the helicopter must be secured, rope messengers must be kept at hand.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>All personnel must be warned to keep clear of rotors and exhausts.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Additional checks/ precautions for tankers:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>All tank openings must be secured.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>If the tanker is not fitted with an inert gas system, pressure from the tanks must be released within 30 minutes of commencement of helicopter operations.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>If the tanker is fitted with an inert gas system, pressure in the cargo tanks must be reduced to slight positive pressure.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Additional checks/ precautions for gas carriers: </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>All precautions must be taken to prevent vapour emission.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Additional checks/ precautions for bulk carriers and combination carriers:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Surface ventilation to dry bulk cargoes must be ceased; all hatch openings must be battened down fully.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Pilot is seated in the starboard side of the helicopter, so he prefers to approach in such a way that his sight is clearer and approach better accordingly with the starboard side closest to the ship during the approach. Also, the helicopter approaches in such a way that it hovers into the relative wind. The vessel ? s course and speed must be steadied during the operations.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Parts of the vessel where there is increased air-flow turbulence like bow areas should not be used for helicopter operations. A flow of air, as free of turbulence as possible, clear of smoke and other visibility restrictions, over the pick-up area is very important. Larger vessels may have areas marked on their decks which are suitable for helicopter operations.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>During the operations, appropriate signals and lights/ shapes must be displayed. If the vessel engaged in helicopter operations is restricted in her ability to manoeuvre, appropriate signals (shapes/lights) must be displayed in addition to the navigation lights.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The various parties involved during the helicopter operations must be briefed before the operations.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Visual Search: Searching visually is the most effective at the end of any method of search as its final locating method even when electronic equipment ? s are used for the initial homing. Effectiveness of a visual search depends on various factors including location of the look-out persons on the search facility (ship or aircraft), scanning/ searching technique and concentration on searching. Every object seen or noise heard during the search must be reported. During the day, look-out persons on a vessel must be placed high during the day and during the night on the bows as far as forward and as low to the water as possible to hear any calls for help and to establish the best night vision. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-left: 0cm; mso-para-margin-left: 0gd; text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; mso-char-indent-count: 0; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Effectiveness of a visual search depends on:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Weather conditions and visibility.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Type of search craft (vessel, aircraft, life-raft or person).</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>State of the sea (calm, choppy or rough).</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Land features (woods, desert, jungle).</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Daytime or night-time Look-out fatigue.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Size and type of the object being searched.</span>\n</p>',NULL,'text',NULL,32767,0,'2026-04-19 03:45:28',1),(391,152,'1. Concept and application of the triangle of fire and explosion','<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>THE TRIANGLE FIRE: CONCEPT AND APPLICATION TO FIRE AND EXPLOSION</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>We all know that from immemorial time fire has been in different planes at the service of man, either to cook your food, keep warm, protected from predators and at present, no more than that, at home, labour, industry, etc.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>It\'s bad when fire escapes our control, and that\'s when we find certain difficulties not only for the loss of our property but the most unfortunate of all losses, life.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Negligence and recklessness and even the lack of knowledge about how to act in certain circumstances are reasons why the fire has claimed many lives.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>At the same time man discovered the secret to light the fire, it changed the course of survival.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Fire shaped the split pots cook and food storage and landed another big evolutionary step in life, the melting of metals.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Fire has been completely dominated by men, but sometimes turns against him. So they created a regulatory use. Thus began almost at the dawn of humanity, the fight against organized fire</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>What is fire?<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes;\"> ? ? </span>Fire is the violent oxidation process of combustible matter, with evolution of flames, heat and gasses. It is a highly exothermic process. When a fire starts, the fire continues until there ? ? s nothing left that can burn.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The fire is a combustion reaction characterized by the emission of heat accompanied by smoke, flames or both.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>1.1 Fire conditions</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>We believe that fires are caused by the action of a heat source powerful enough to initiate combustion.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>These causes can classify them as follows:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Electric Causes:</span></strong><span> ? short circuit, arc current, overheating.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Friction:</span></strong><span> ? heating by friction.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Naked flames:</span></strong><span> ? candles, wicks and matches in a state of ignition.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>No</span></strong><span> ? <strong>sparks or combustion:</strong> ? (satellites) sparks and embers from combustion of solids.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Welding:</span></strong><span> ? When acetylene, prevention and carelessly used.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; mso-add-space: auto; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 18.0pt;\">\n</a><!-- [if mso & !supportInlineShapes & supportFields]><span lang=EN-US><span style=\'mso-spacerun:yes\'> ? </span>SHAPE \\* MERGEFORMAT <![endif]--> <!-- [if mso & !supportInlineShapes & supportFields]><span lang=EN-US><v:shape id=\"_x0000_i1025\" type=\"#_x0000_t75\" style=\'width:.85pt; height:.85pt\'> <v:imagedata croptop=\"-65520f\" cropbottom=\"65520f\"/> </v:shape><![endif]-->\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Hot surfaces:</span></strong><span> ? plates, motors, hot water heaters.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Electricity:</span></strong><span> ? generated by systems involving friction.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Challenged minds:</span></strong><span> ? pyro maniacs</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>1.2 Properties of flammable materials</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Process by which a substance combines with oxygen. ? In this process, it is normal that discards energy as heat.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Has sometimes slow oxidation, for example oxidation of iron or wood disturbance. ? At other times, the oxidation is ultra-fast; ? for example, in an explosion. ? Fire is an example of a quick, oxidation energy as light, heat and flames.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>FUELS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<span>A substance in the presence of oxygen and giving it certain activation energy, is able to burn. ? Fuels may be classified according to the armature:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%281%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>In accordance with the above definition overall, it was necessary, for better study them, make a subdivision considering the physical condition that may present, being the same as follows:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Solid Fuels</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Liquid Fuels</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Gaseous Fuels</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: 0cm; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l0 level2 lfo1; tab-stops: list 72.0pt; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 72.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%282%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>SOLID FUELS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Most solid fuels are cellulosic materials such as natural wood, paper, cardboard, fibre based composites, and textiles, especially those of vegetable origin.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>When the material is eventually subdivided, the danger of initiation and / or propagation of fire is much larger; ? for example, when it is in the form of dust, dirt or lint.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>SPEED COMBUSTION</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>The burning rate of a solid fuel depends on your setup. ? If powder or chips burns much faster than if it is in the form of blocks. ? This is because when a solid fuel is divided into small pieces can absorb much more rapidly heat and vaporize faster.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -144.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 144.0pt;\">\n<span>A special form of finely divided solid fuel in</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Particulates are clouds of combustible material, ex. dust clouds produced by a load of grain (wheat, corn, soy, etc).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>When one of these clouds reaches the proper temperature burns very quickly, sometimes looking like an explosion as happened on ships were loading or unloading grain.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>LIQUID FUELS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Flammable liquids are widely used in various activities, and negligent or improper use causes many fires.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Liquids do not burn; ? the burning are vapours arising from them. ? These vapours are generally heavier than air and may ignite a considerable distance from the broadcast source. A variety of flammable liquids, used nowadays in various activities. ? The most common are: Naphtha, benzene, kerosene, alcohol, cellulose solutions and thinners.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>The heavier liquid fuels, such as oil, do not burn at ordinary temperatures, but when heated they give off vapours ? that are progressively favouring the possibility of combustion, which reaches a high enough temperature to concentrate.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Those who are always on board a ship are: Fuel - oil (bunker); ? Diesel - oil; ? lubricating oil; ? fats; ? kerosene; ? oil-based paints; ? paint thinner. ? Combustible liquid cargoes are crude oils, petroleum products and chemicals. ? Liquefied gases should also be included among the flammable liquids.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>MIST OF LIQUID FUELS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Liquid fuel off gas (vapour) at a speed higher solid fuels because the liquid molecules are looser than those of the solids. ? Besides, producing gases liquids in a large temperature range.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>For example, gasoline begins to give off flammable gases at a temperature of 43 degrees C. This means that gasoline is always dangerous to ambient temperatures. ? If in spite of this heated gas, gassing in much greater quantity.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Heavier than gasoline, liquid fuels are the fuel - oil or lubricating oils, just off the gases if heated. ? Example, certain lubricants reach only fire if placed at temperatures of 204 degrees C.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Should be noted that this temperature, although not a room temperature is very low in case of fire, so that the heavy liquid fuel, ignition rapidly enter when they are within range of normal heat generated by a fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The gas (vapour) which shows a liquid fuel is heavier than air, which turns the gas into a major danger.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The flammable gas travels seeking the lower places is not easily dissipated and move to reach considerable distances from the produced liquid fuel. ? If an ignition source located in the displacement, such as an electric spark produced by an electric motor, or not protected switch will immediately arise shaped combustion explosion.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>When a tank is filled with a liquid fuel, or a loss occurs in a pipe or hose spilled liquid usually extends over a large area. ? Immediately begins to release much flammable gas that perfectly mixes with oxygen. ? If an ignition source appears, will emerge combustion generally violent and widespread form.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>GASEOUS FUELS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The most common flammable gases are hydrogen, acetylene, butane, propane, etc. ? Are all hot to an atmosphere of air or oxygen. ? However, a non-flammable gas, such as chlorine, can ignite in an atmosphere of hydrogen. ? Conversely, a flammable gas does not burn to an atmosphere of carbon dioxide or nitrogen.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%283%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Among the non-combustible gases are two classes: those which act as oxidizing, ex. that allow combustion and tend to suppress it. ? Oxidizing gases containing different proportions of oxygen and suppressing the combustion are known as inert gases, the most common nitrogen, helium, carbon dioxide and argon.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Apart from the above, it is appropriate to note that the fire also produces gases, many of which are combustible.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>On board usually acetylene and propane and butane gas for domestic use are often in ships employed in the welding gas.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>SPEED COMBUSTION OF GASEOUS FUELS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Gaseous fuels are already in a gaseous state. ? To burn only need to be mixed with oxygen and an ignition source. ? In most cases it is very likely that the oxygen in the air and so is very close to the gas. ? For this reason, the flammable gases always represent a significant hazard.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>COMBUSTION</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>A substance whose presence in fuel burn. ? In general, it is considered as a typical oxygen oxidizer. ? It is in the air at a concentration of 21% by volume.</span>\n</p>\n\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%284%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>There are others, such as choleric acid, ozone, hydrogen peroxide, etc. Fuels having a high number of oxygen atoms in the molecule does not need to burn combustion (organic peroxides).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>ENERGY ACTIVATION</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Is the energy required to initiate the reaction?</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Ignition sources that provide this energy include: electrical overload or short circuit, friction between metal parts, welding equipment, stoves, chemical reactions, sparks, etc.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>TYPES AND SOURCES OF IGNITION</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>EMERGING STAGE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>It is characterized by no flames, no little smoke, the temperature is low; ? large amount of combustion particles are generated. ? These particles are invisible and behave like gases, climbing onto the roof. ? This stage can last for days, weeks and years (Sequoia tree in California, a person whose trunk threw a lit cigarette was in this stage for three years).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>LATENT STAGE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Still no significant heat or flame; ? begins to increase the number of particles to be visible; ? Now the particles are called smoke. ? The duration of this stage is also variable.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>STAGE CALL</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>According to the fire develops, the ignition point is reached and starts the flames. ? Lower the amount of smoke and heat rises. ? Its length can vary, but generally the fourth stage takes place in seconds.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>STAGE HEAT</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>At this stage a lot of heat, flames, smoke and toxic gases are generated.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%285%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>1.3 Principles for fighting fires</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Are varied extinguishing agents used in laptops, so, it also changed the degree of effectiveness of each of them and the limitations in their applicability. ? They can be classified into:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 18.0pt;\">\n</a><!-- [if mso & !supportInlineShapes & supportFields]><span lang=EN-US><span style=\'mso-spacerun:yes\'> ? </span>SHAPE \\* MERGEFORMAT <![endif]--> <!-- [if mso & !supportInlineShapes & supportFields]><span lang=EN-US><v:shape id=\"_x0000_i1025\" type=\"#_x0000_t75\" style=\'width:.85pt; height:.85pt\'> <v:imagedata croptop=\"-65520f\" cropbottom=\"65520f\"/> </v:shape><![endif]-->\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 18.0pt;\">\nv\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>WATER</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>For its abundance it ? ? s the most common in fire fighting operations. ? Your extinguisher action is based on cooling the burning material. ? Is applied in the form of a pressurized jet, or as a fine spray over the entire burning surface. ? It is well, thrown in large quantities through hoses or sprinklers on relatively small amounts of oil.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 18.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%286%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>This extinguishing agent can only be applied in the Class \"A\" fires, and in some cases fires CLASS \"B\" as the oil fires, which should be applied in the manner described. ? Never use in fires CLASS \"C\", and even less in the Class \"D\" fires.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>FOAM</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>The foam may be produced by mixing a solution of aluminium sulphate with other sodium bicarbonate and adding a stabilizer. ? In laptops foam is produced by the reaction of the two mentioned solutions, resulting in an internal pressure resulting from carbon dioxide generation, capable of impelling the foam to a distance of seven (7) feet.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Foam remove oxygen to form a layer which prevents the passage of air, and also cools somewhat. ? Is applied as a layer covering the surface of the burning liquid. ? However, in many cases, the fumes arising from burning substances pass through the foam layer, and if the concentration is sufficient, it will burn up.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%287%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>It is especially useful in extinguishing class B fires, and where the smothering effect of extinguishing agent is of great importance. ? Certain solvents (alcohols, acetone, etc.) rolled foam, which is not convenient to use it on fire of these substances. ? Being electrically conductive should never be used on Class C fires also is contraindicated in Class D. Fires</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>CARBON DIOXIDE OR DI CO2</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt -7.1pt;\">\n<span>This gas can be stored under pressure in portable extinguishers and discharged through a special nozzle on the site where it is needed. ? The property of the carbon dioxide extinguisher is its smothering effect accompanied by a slight cooling. ? Do not use in confined or poorly ventilated areas, as the user can be subject to mechanical asphyxia due to lack of oxygen, it is suitable for Class B fires and Class C. Not suitable for other types of fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt -7.1pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%288%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>CHEMICAL DRY POWDER </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>The products are mixtures of finely pulverized combustible, such as Sodium Carbonate, Sodium Bicarbonate, Sodium Sulphate, Sodium Silicate, Bentonite, etc. Works by drowning as it applies seeking form a layer on the burning material.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>In laptops the extinguishing agent is ejected by the pressure produced by a nitrogen capsule, located inside the fire extinguisher and which is broken at the time of use.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%289%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>According to the composition of the mixture, the dry chemical is suitable for Class A, B fires, and C. Not suitable for Class D fires</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>Flammable materials commonly used on board</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Rags soaked in vegetable oil or paints can cause spontaneous combustion. ? In these cases, fire prevention mainly consists of good habits of cleanliness and order. There are other materials that normally do not produce spontaneous combustion but that under certain conditions if they can produce. Among these are wood, which like other substances, has to be heated to emit flammable gases that can burn, most pipes water vapour does not have such a high temperature to burn wood.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>When a piece of wood in contact with a steam pipe or a similar source of low temperature, the effect is that the wood is becoming charcoal, wood Also burning reaching to below the timber temperature and that heat pipe vapor may ignite.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Transforming wood charcoal may take several days, it is easy for the process to go unnoticed without anyone noticing until it becomes the first signs of fire caused.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>This type of fire can be prevented by keeping combustibles or well away from heat sources. ? If this is not possible, protect combustible substances by heat insulating materials.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>FUEL CHARGES</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Found powdered metals such as magnesium, titanium, calcium and zirconium are rapidly oxidized in air with moisture and give off heat which is sometimes enough to burn.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Chlorine reacts voluntarily if it comes in contact with certain metals or organics. ? Sodium and potassium react strongly with water.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Dry metal shavings are not prone to spontaneous combustion when these metal particles are stained with oil is very easy to reach Spontaneous combustion, and this is due to the oxidation of the oil and is very easy to reach Spontaneous combustion . ? Occurs frequently in vessels carrying these cargoes, opening warehouses to download it enters the air oxygen can produce slightly intense flames.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Coals may ignite spontaneously depending on the moisture content, if found in particles or blocks, and some alternative causes. ? <strong> ? </strong> ? Many goods that can ignite spontaneously are:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Alfalfa meal</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Charcoal</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Cod liver oil</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Canvas</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Compound corn meal</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Fishmeal</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Fish Oils</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Linseed oil</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Fabrics</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Oiled</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>A measure taken to prevent spontaneous combustion is to separate the fibrous materials of oils.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>ELECTRICAL EQUIPMENT AND DEFECTIVE CIRCUIT</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The electrical fires claim the lives of many people every year and injuring others. ? Some of these fires are caused by faults in the electrical system and defects in electrical appliances, but many more are caused by misuse and neglect of electrical appliances, incorrectly installed wiring and overloaded circuits and extension cords.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Electricity is an energy source for many applications, considering that teams and circuits are properly insulated and protected. ? When using electrical equipment incorrectly, or have lost their drivers insulation, excessive wear, become serious fire hazards. ? Electrical equipment should be installed, tested and repaired in strict accordance with safety standards and only by qualified personnel.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>ELECTRICAL</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Electrical equipment permanently installed I deal is subject to a lot of wear due to salic air causes corrosion and vibration of the ship breaks the isolation and some pieces of equipment, which results in the electrical equipment or conductors can produce sparks that are excellent sources of ignition where flammable gases in the vicinity.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The electrical equipment installed on ships should be of special design, provide maintenance and should only be used for the purpose for which it has been placed. ? The person is not suitable or no knowledge of how to use it should help.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>CABLES AND FUSE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The electrical wiring causes twice as many fires from causing electrical appliances. ? When activities are indoors and increases the use of lighting, heating and appliances. Most fires caused by electrical cables begin in cabins or engine rooms</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2810%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>v</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>touch, a short circuit occurs. ? Or it can produce a spark, that most cases has enough energy to initiate combustion if flammable gases.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>The fuses or breakers are</span></strong><span> ? also delicate since there many times over the needed breakers or fuses are put inappropriate. ? One and others leave more current due to pass and can heat the entire circuit. ? There have been cases where entire system begins to burn and with it, the flammable materials are near.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The most effective way to prevent fires from these causes have to replace cables and contacts who have lost insulation and should be placed solely the fuses and breakers of electrical circuits appropriate to their size immediately.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>ELECTRICAL OVERLOAD</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Power surges as it flows through a circuit a current of very high intensity, these devices operate automatically interrupting the passage of electric current; ? otherwise could lead wires heating appliances and impair or may even cause an accident by fire. ? Sometimes in cabins and deposits, with one outlet stream, energy is taken to individual applications, such as radios, fans, tape, irons, heaters, television, etc. ? and this can be dangerous because the wires of each outlet are calculated for the specific electrical charge. ? If this value exceeds the wires are heated and can get burned, communicating its heat to combustible materials that may be in the vicinity causing a fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>ELECTRIC LIGHT BULBS OF UNPROTECTED</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The heat from an electric bulb may burn combustible materials in contact with the bulb. ? In vessels were given fire due to balance, since for example plastic curtains come in contact with an ignition source and start, causing major consequences. ? In decks that are outdoors, there are times that sources are protected by a plastic sleeve or canvas, which is an excellent measure of protection when such lights are off. But if the case is allowed to focus on, is very likely that heat lamp to burn arrives just about covers portable lamps used to illuminate a job in a low-light area. ? These portable lights give off heat, and can fall over, especially in bad weather, so that the filament of the bulb can make a spark with another metal part of the ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>ELECTRIC MOTORS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>They are a common cause of fires on board. ? If this does not adequately maintained or when working beyond their capacity or useful life, is very likely to cause problems. ? Electric motors require regular inspection, testing, cleaning and lubrication. ? If one of the windings is shorted or has a ground wire can make the engine bearing is overheating. ? In any case, it is an ignition source, which in the circumstances may be the source of a serious fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>STATIC ELECTRICITY</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>It is the excess electrical charge accumulating certain materials, usually by friction (Ex. by rubbing a pen with a human hair), and you cannot escape them.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The generation of electrostatic charges is a natural phenomenon, associated with the atomic structure of matter itself, which occurs as a result of relative motion between two surfaces in contact, usually of different substances, whether liquid or solid, of which, or the two is not a good conductor of electricity. ? There are two fundamental processes of training loads: the contact-separation of substances and friction.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Static electricity is a temporary imbalance in the distribution of charges on the surface of two materials in contact by electron transfer, creating an electric field and a difference in potential between those which can be very high.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The magnitude of the charge depends primarily on the rate of separation and / or friction of the material and its electrical resistivity.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Other parameters such as the oxidation state of the rubbing surface, the presence of water and immiscible as iron oxide particles, the nature of the metal containers and pipes, the influence of temperature, etc. ? are also important for load generation and its polarity.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>When conducting bodies are separated by an insulating or even air constitute a capacitor to be charged one with a positive charge and one with another load equal but negative. ? By establishing a conducting path such discharging stored energy is released and possibly producing a spark. ? It is this sudden recombination by spark of the separated charges constituting the risk.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Generally such spark flashovers technically called, are produced through an electrically charged air between the body and a proximal body uncharged, but electrically connected to earth, to be either a very short distance. ? A smaller distance is also smaller than the voltage necessary to produce the spark.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The fundamental parameter determining the danger of a spark is the amount of energy released at time occurs. ? This energy is manifested in the form of radiation, (making visible spark), ionization and heat. ? Schematically is the latter triggered the combustion reaction.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>When such electrostatic discharge spark is produced in a flammable atmosphere is relatively easy that the fire starts, since activation energy that contribute usually be higher than is required for combustion of gases and vapours ? , usually of order of 0.25 mJ.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The ignition hazard exists when the spark is generated by a potential difference greater than 1,000 V.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>For fires or explosions occur the following three conditions must together be met:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The existence of a fuel oxidizer mixture or capable of explosion or fire for being within the flammable range.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Accumulating a sufficiently high electrostatic charge to create a potential difference generating spark.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The production of electrostatic discharge (spark) primer, sufficient to ignite a dangerous mix energy.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Static electricity is an ignition source of the utmost importance especially tankers. ? It is especially dangerous because it is not easy to know when electrical charges sufficient condition to make a spark.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>In tankers are substances that rub product loading or unloading on one side and the pipes, valves, pumps and tanks on the other.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The best way to prevent the accumulation of electrostatic charges is electrically connect the dock facilities and ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>BATTERY CHARGE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>By putting loads or discharge batteries emit hydrogen, a highly flammable gas. ? A mixture of air with an amount of hydrogen ranging from 4.1% to 74.2% by volume, is potentially explosive.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Hydrogen is lighter than air and therefore tends to rise. ? If there is no ventilation, hydrogen becomes concentrated in the upper part of the enclosure where the batteries, and if there is adequate ventilation, hydrogen becomes concentrated in the upper part of the enclosure where the batteries, and the emergence of a source of ignition will occur immediately explosion.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The best way to avoid this risk is adequately ventilate the area where the batteries are, and strictly prohibit smoking and not allowing any machine work sparking or overheating.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>Lockers AND DEPOSITS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>When you are on board the ship, and for reasons of haste or struggling to get the supplies are kept in lockers and balances \"provisional\" way, hoping for a later time the proper stowage of such provisions, and there are times that later time never comes and the ship sets sail with their belongings and badly stowed provisions. ? When you begin nodding and balances objects fall, hit, mixed, sometimes breaking electrical circuits or foci, and even give pipes where leaks occur. ? All this means that fire starts occur, due to the nature of most of the things that are stored in the lockers of ships, is very easy to reach serious proportions.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The best way to avoid this is to stow and store things away.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>KITCHEN</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The kitchen and its dependencies in most of the ships are very busy places, there is usually a lot of activity, there are several people working on a not very large, there is less abundant flame or heat build-up of fat and other combustible materials. ? The kitchen is considered a dangerous place.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>POWER SOURCE FOR COOKING</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>In most boats power source most used in kitchens is electricity, occasional diesel stoves used - oil, although less often. ? In small vessels regularly cooked with propane or butane gas.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The kitchen appliances are also subject to take precautions such as electrical equipment from the rest of the ship, although they are more dangerous because they are more likely to have contact with water. ? If liquid fuels are used, the staff working in the kitchen should be aware of the leak, pipes and fittings produced by mishandling or damage caused by items falling on them. ? If a loss of fuel is discovered, it should immediately close the inlet valves, report the fault and repair it before resuming use.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>STOVE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The heat produced by the stove can cause a fire in the kitchen so it becomes a dangerous device. ? Fuel may increase its fire originated elsewhere.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Those working in the kitchen should consider not leave cloths, towels, rags or paper near the stove if it is on or off newborn. Plus it should not be any kind material over a stove. Which are not suitable for cooking? ? You should try to have heaters in good working order with pilot lights in order and gas regulators in good condition so that they may know from afar when the burners are connected.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>ELECTRIC FRYER</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>These devices are quite used in the kitchens of the ship and are another source of potential fires. ? It should be used with caution and supervision.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>These should be subject to fixed parts of the ship preventing slipping when the vessel balances or halters. ? As a precaution we must take into account that should never be put in a hot wet food fryer. And should not be overfilled to prevent hot oil from spilling and causing a fire, like a fryer should never be left alone, always Kitchen staff must be vigilant.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Never move a pan that is on fire, and you can burn and dump all the oil. ? Never attempt to turn off the oil with boiling water because a burst of smoke that would rekindle the fire may occur. ? In a deep fryer can be deadly because: water is a conductor of electricity and you would stay \"stuck\", or unplug electric current cut it. ? What you have to do is cover the pan with a lid that is larger than the pan itself, and so leave it until it goes off.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Do not use defective appliances or electrical equipment in disrepair.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>1.4 Fire propagation</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>All substances in the universe is in one of three states: solid, liquid or gas (steam). ? Molecules and atoms are directly bonded solid and secured together. ? In liquids, the molecules and atoms are attached, but not so tight.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>In a gas or vapour molecules can move freely, because the binding forces between them is weak. ? For the existence of an oxidation reaction is necessary that the molecules of the body to be oxidized are completely surrounded by oxygen molecules. ? This is not possible in the solid or liquid, as its molecules are packed around or upon and it is not possible to enter the oxygen molecules inside the solid or liquid. ? However, it is very easy to completely surround the oxygen molecules of gases. ? For this reason alone gases (fumes) can burn.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n</a><!-- [if mso & !supportInlineShapes & supportFields]><span lang=EN-US><span style=\'mso-spacerun:yes\'> ? </span>SHAPE \\* MERGEFORMAT <![endif]--> <!-- [if mso & !supportInlineShapes & supportFields]><span lang=EN-US><v:shape id=\"_x0000_i1025\" type=\"#_x0000_t75\" style=\'width:.85pt; height:.85pt\'> <v:imagedata croptop=\"-65520f\" cropbottom=\"65520f\"/> </v:shape><![endif]-->\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2811%29.png\">\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>When a solid or a liquid is heated, its molecules begin to move, when more heat is applied, the faster they move, if sufficient heat applied, some molecules break their bonds with other molecules allying the body and forming a gas (vapour) and is able to oxidize and therefore able to burn. ? If heated to the ignition temperature and sufficient oxygen is present, the gas (steam) is oxidized rapidly, resulting in combustion.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Burning is the rapid oxidation of millions of molecules of gas (vapour). ? When burned, the molecules are broken into atoms and these are recombined with oxygen to form new molecules. ? During the breakup and recombination of molecules is when energy is released as heat and light.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The heat released is \"radiant heat\" which is pure energy of the same kind that radiated by the sun. ? Or travelling is radiated in all directions. ? Some of this energy heats the substance itself, EX. the fuel. ? This radiation causes the fuel to detach more gas (vapour) and the temperature of this gas reaches its ignition temperature. ? At the same time, the next fire heats air, which creates a slight atmospheric depression so that more air to reach the flames. ? The result is that the gas (vapour) newly formed begins to burn and increases fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>1.5 Safety practices</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>A fire can be assumed to be controlled when:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The extinguishing agent is applied to the base of the fire and was able to penetrate into the innermost part of the fire already beginning to cool.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>It has obscured the main body of fire. ? In this condition, the fire cannot generate enough heat to re-ignite a fuel that is near.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>They have been examined and protected all possible routes of spread of fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Has been completed preliminary search of victims.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>FINALIZATION OPERATION OF FIRE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Before you can declare the termination of the operation of fire, Captain of the ship should have safety, confirmed by the foreman of fire, one who has performed at the scene of the fire, as verified points essential, such as:</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>A COMPREHENSIVE REVIEW OF THE WHOLE AREA FIRE FOR SURE:</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>All routes of spread of fire have been examined and controlled.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>You have reached the level of protection necessary and that all gases and smoke are vented to the atmosphere.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The burned area is already safe so that you can enter without resuscitation equipment</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>We have already done a full review of all materials burned.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>It has established a guard may re - awakening the fire. ? A crew member, or more than one if the fire has been great, must be present at the fire scene to give notice in the event of a resumption of the fire place.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>It has recovered and restored all fire equipment that has been used. ? Hoses used have been collected, or replaced with new. ? All the material has been properly cleaned, dried and ready for storage. ? Portable fire extinguishers are recharged and placed on their sites. ? Equipment for artificial respiration have been cleaned and ready for reuse.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>There has been damage control, proceeding to a thorough examination to determine the damages suffered by the people and the ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>It has made a count of all shipboard personnel for possible victims.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>POS-REVIEW OF THE FIRE PRODUCES</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Shortly after the transaction is completed and that all fire fighting equipment has been recovered and put into service, a review of the operation should be performed. Criticism does not have to be a very formal and solemn ceremony actually may be appropriate criticism while taking a cup of coffee before returning to the normal duties of board.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Criticism can begin by recalling that the crew just put out a fire on a ship that is in the sea, which is a great success and a source of satisfaction. ? However, while the details of the operation are still fresh in everyone\'s mind, it is necessary to consider certain questions? ? How could have done better? ? More important? ? How could you have prevented it had appeared the fire? ? If you had to redo the operation of fire tomorrow? ? It would be the same way? ? He could have gotten the same results with less fatigue and less physical wear gang fire and with less damage to the ship?</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>While you are discussing all this, the Officer in charge of fire safety should be encouraged to make suggestions and recommendations are formulated and should write them down. ? The ideas that are useful to include in the new plan fire on board.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>1.6 Security of continuous surveillance</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>VIGILANT SERVICES BOARD</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The surveillance is carried out by people detection and fire alarm, analogous to the automatic system. ? The biggest difference between the system of people and automatic monitoring is how is done.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>BOARD DUTIES OF VIGILANT AGAINST ON-FIRE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>A watchman on board may have other specific requirements; we refer only to relations with the detection and fire alarm.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The guards should receive detailed instructions and comply with its obligations to perfection. ? It is necessary to instruct his fundamental duty is to transmit the alarm in case of fire, even without seeing the fire, only with b presence of smoke or odour. ? There have been occasions when they have wasted precious time when the guard has stepped up to the navigation bridge to report the alarm rather than activate it from a fire box.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>After transmitting the alarm the guard will take the following actions: wake the passengers and crew of the affected area, use a fire extinguisher or simply report it to the duty officer. ? For further investigation of the fire, it is important that the caretaker who has discovered a fire make a brief report stating: time and date of the fire, where the exact location of fire or smoke was seen for the first time, found doors they were closed or opened, who was in the area before discovering and fire extinguishers if worked properly and any other details that helps to research the causes and effects of fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>1.7 Patrol systems</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The rest of the crew staff to perform other specific tasks. ? Some of the gang members to act as researchers. ? Under the protection of water from the hose, should be investigating the possibility that staff has been consciously or unconsciously trapped. ? Other members of the crew must investigate if the fire spreads to other regions or to better identify the area of ? ? fire or act as messengers if necessary.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 150%;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>1.8 Fire hazards</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The causes of fire are many among them we can mention:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>WHAT CAUSES THERMIC FIRES </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Over loaded facilities.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Damage to electrical equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Lack of control on open flames or other heat sources.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Place fuel elements near sources of heat or flame.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2812%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-margin-top-alt: auto; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>Human causes</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Smoking in unauthorized places</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Throw matches or cigarette butts carelessly</a></span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Lack of housekeeping</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2813%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Flammable products should not be located near heat sources such as heaters, stoves, lighters, radiators or sunlight.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The friction of containers or other metal materials may cause sparks, even if they are invisible.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Avoid, also, the static sparking putting media for download.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Generally when a fire occurs in the laboratory usually at hand there are fire extinguishers; so performance is fast enough, the extinguisher must be located in an easily accessible place, at the end of the bench. ? The suitability of the material for extinction depends on ignited substance, but commonly CO2 extinguishers are the most practical and universal.</span>\n</p>',NULL,'text',NULL,32767,0,'2026-04-19 03:45:28',1),(392,152,'2. Firefighting organization','<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Fires on ships are the most difficult fire to control because of the wide variety of fuel are on board and many in which the products of combustion can hinder fire fighting operations.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Addition, vessel design greatly complicates extinction. For example, when the fire is located in an apartment below deck, surrounded by steel bulkheads and decks, this fire is difficult, if not impossible, to achieve, and air combat. It may also be possible to achieve the materials that are burning in the bottom of a cargo hold as it would have to be removed everything that is stowed above, it can be nearly impossible, especially if the ship is at sea.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>An important question is how should strike fire crew at a given location on a ship?</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.1 General Alarm Emergency</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The crew member, who discovers a fire, or an indication that there is a fire, activates the alarm or report them quickly scrub. Delaying this can cause a small fire from becoming a big fire. Once a winning intensity fire spreads rapidly.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>All fires should be reported to superiors, even those small fires that become extinct themselves. Each fire should be investigated to find out what were the defects or faults that have allowed him to suffer the fire to correct them and to not reappear in the future.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.2 Fire control plans and master list</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>It is a mandatory document required by the International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea (SOLAS) to be exposed in a boat at various points visible. It will be easily understood, illustrated instructions take all possible cases and shall be written in language that all crew members can understand.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Table of Notes is a list of all specifics to each of the members of the tasks crew during emergencies.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Table of Notes consists of multiple columns. The first column, usually the left, is the one that contains the list of the ship\'s crew. No names appear as these may vary, but appear numbers and positions of the crew. It is the duty of every crew knows exactly what the number as shown in the Table of Duties.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Although Tables Obligations may differ from one boat to another, in general terms all contain similar information. Below will give a brief description of the information contained in a box Obligations of a general cargo ship.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>To the right of the column shown is the relationship of the crew, a column appears with the distribution of functions and signals in case of fire. In the event of a fire signal is a sequence of bells toll no less than 10 seconds duration supplemented with a continuous sound alarm bells.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The removal of this signal is 3 short emergencies painted the ship\'s whistle, the signal supplemented with 3 long sounds produced alarm bells.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The call signal for fire drills is a prior verbal warning to the crew and the signs are the same as in the case of call and recall of fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The distribution functions of each crew set their specific performance during fire emergency tasks. Thus, only by way of example, the 2nd. Officer is required to bring documentation and money on the ship in a safe place and then report it to the 1st. Officer. If injured, you will practice the necessary first aids and then report to the 1st officer. Another example, and Sailor No.2 gourd all inlets, dining vents and cowls, and other openings through which airflow can reach the fire. Then handle the portable extinguisher not the 1st Official orders.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>One more example. The Kitchen Assistant has the following tasks. He will take the place of the fire extinguisher No.8. He closes the doors and gates of the upper decks.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Specific tasks and signals for the case that the ship is in danger also appears in Table Operations such as that caused by<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes;\"> ? </span>grounding, collision, bad weather that might jeopardizes the ship, etc.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>There is also another column where signals and specific duties in the event of vessel abandonment appear. Finally the last column records the distribution of the crew in lifeboats giving specific tasks and the number of boat which must go.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Obviously, all crew members must be thoroughly familiar with their specific duties in an emergency and know them without resourcing to manuals when the emergency has arisen.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.3 Communications</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Communication with the head, usually the master of the vessel should be made through phone or messenger. Communications with the fire crew should also be established and maintained. Messengers can be the most effective for this purpose, because the telephone lines can be destroyed by the same fire and it is normal that the members of the fire crew are constantly moving. Appropriate radio system could also be used.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.4 Staff Safety Procedures</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>SOLAS establishes the need to do periodic fire drills, as previously mentioned. But not only it is an obligation under an agreement, but it is the principle which says better safe than sorry. An emergency may not be a common occurrence, but has emerged cu justifies all the effort that has been made in the preparation and training. So take all the crew with the greatest interest, the maximum application and greater efforts need to sound properly prepare for possible emergencies on board required. If these ever arise, nothing is lost. But in the case that has arisen and has not been properly prepared, the losses in lives and property damage can be extremely high and beyond justification.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.5 Periodic drills on board</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>It is very important to know the procedures and drills on fire prevention</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>It is always important in case of fire to take into account proper protective gear and implements. ? As this would avoid loss of crew us, and accidents thereof.</span>\n</p>\n\n<img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image.png\">\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>It is important to have of all the equipment in good conditions such as spraying hoses for safety. ALL Crew members must be trained to use it, in case of fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%281%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Evacuate in an orderly and quiet fashion, all the lifeboats, the fastest way to avoid any kind of tragedy happens at the time of fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%282%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>LOCATION OF FIRE EQUIPMENT AND EVACUATION ROUTES IN AN EMERGENCY</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Fire Organization</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The plans are part of business management of fire risk.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The fire organization has two objectives:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Minimize the number of fire emergencies.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Check quickly that emergencies consequences are minimal.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Given a particular risk, the plan or fire plans can be contained as human planning and organizing for the optimal use of technical means provided, in order to minimize the potential economic and human consequences of the emergency.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>Time Aspect</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>As already mentioned above, in an emergency a number of actions are taken to limit their consequences: Evacuate, try extinguishing own, warn fire fighters, etc. ? A key to the success of these actions is to remember that any action to be taken involves a delay, during which the emergency has been developed and its control becomes increasingly more difficult, The NTP are guides good practices. ? Its indications are not binding unless they are included in an existing statutory provision. ? In order to assess the relevance of the recommendations contained in a particular NTP is appropriate to note the date of publication.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>Weather emergency intervention</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Before reaching the point of intervention to detect an inverted elapse time the fire alarm the people to be involved and that such persons be prepared and prepare appropriate means indicated as an example of what we will discuss a very common aspect in all plans: the evacuation.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>It led to a number N of people to evacuate in a given situation, no one will evacuate to an inverted time has elapsed:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Detect fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Alarm people to be evacuated.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Identify the escape route where should be directed to evacuate and access.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The times of detection, alarm and evacuation are links in a chain. ? The network may fail at the weakest link and if the plan fails.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.6 Shipbuilding provisions</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Characteristics accordingly done thru a blueprint,<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes;\"> ? ? </span>includes the hull, rigging and engine installations and auxiliary services. After the shipyard starts construction. If it is a small boat hull and deck in plastic resins are reinforced with fibre glass first and then the gel coat (white cover color) then stay abroad. Thereon layers of glass fibres are embedded in epoxy resin (= \"two-component\") to achieve the thickness range. In some cases are reinforcements are incorporated. Using the same method, the deck is made. The interior areas are then finished at dry dock.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>There the boat passes the part of enlistment, where all facilities, engines, electrical cables, the comfort elements are placed. Finally all elements of decoration (curtains, cushions) are placed. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>If instead it is a ship, modern shipyards are constructing modules hull (bow, the part of wineries, the aft with deckhouse and engine). Finally huge cranes together modules that bind with new welds to finally form the vessel. The ships also have a recruitment phase but usually occur after launching. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The timing of the launch is the big moment when the boat is lowered into the water. In smaller vessels usually already all the details are finished, while big ships are released and then are completed afloat.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Finally sea trials, where features confirming all systems are tested to ensure proper operation. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>And then comes to her boss.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.7 Emergency fire pump (Cargo ships)</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Fire pumps</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>One of the main differences between a ground fire and a fire in the harbour, is the source of extinguishing agent; is water, it is inexhaustible, contrary to what occurs in a truck at the first output that we will have approximately 10,000 litres of water in the best case. This is certainly an advantage, but as we have never insisted should become a factor adverse to our security. Fire pumps vessels can provide high flow rates, but not to lift heavy pressure, one of the reasons that discouraged the use of hoses 25 mm, which produce large losses in output pressure not very high (about 7lbs / cm2) .</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Sanitary pumps, ballast, bilge or general service can be considered as fire pumps, provided that they are not normally used for pumping oil and that if intended occasionally transfer or pumping of oil fuel, are endowed Suitable devices changes.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The ship shall carry at least two fire pumps operated by power, and passenger ships gross arch or above 4000 at least three. In vessels if fire declared in any one compartment could put all the pumps is declared, no other consistent means an emergency fire pump complying in Systems Code Fire Safety, with its power source and connection. Sea located outside the space where the main fire pumps and their sources of power which shall be independently driven. Usually the power source is a diesel pump and fuel tank service engine contains a sufficient amount of fuel for the pump to operate at full load for at least three hours.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Access to emergency pump must be safe from foreign and remote decks the machinery space containing the main fire pumps, located in a well-ventilated place so that it cannot be taken out of service due to smoke or flood water.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The diameter of the fire main and fire piping is necessary for the effective distribution of the maximum quantity of water required in respect of two fire pumps operating simultaneously, except in the case of cargo, in which case it is sufficient if the diameter is sufficient to a water flow rate of 140 m3 / h.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>It has isolation valves designed to separate from the rest of the fire main section thereof, located within the machinery space in the pump or the main fire pumps are located, and will be installed in a place readily accessible and safe from hazards, outside the machinery spaces. The fire main is so arranged that when the isolating valves are closed, water can be supplied to all the hydrants on the ship, except the machinery space referred to above, by another fire pump or by an emergency fire pump.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>They have a valve for each fire hose so that when the fire pumps are operating can disconnect any hoses.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.8 Chemical powder applicators</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>DRY CHEMICAL</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The products are mixtures of finely pulverized combustible, such as Sodium Carbonate, Sodium Bicarbonate, Sodium Sulphate, Sodium Silicate, Bentonite, etc. ? Works by drowning as it applies attempting to form a layer on the burning material.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>In laptops the extinguishing agent is ejected by the pressure produced by a nitrogen capsule, located inside the fire extinguisher and which is broken at the time of use.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%283%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>According to the composition of the mixture, the dry chemical is suitable for Class A, B fires, and C. Not suitable for Class D fires.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.9 Emergency Escape Routes</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>We offer a range of signs and pictograms for ? standard evacuation. ? All conform to the NTP399.010 rule determining dimensions, colors and pictograms for marking up escape routes under fire safety or claims. ? They facilitate the location of evacuation routes, fire fighting equipment, or safety areas since its luminance can last for more than 4 hours ? and surpassing all standards. ? In an earthquake the power sources are often ? switched off automatically to prevent further hazards; ? establishing a well signposted can save many lives. ? The photo luminescent signs are ideal for buildings, Mines, Hotels, Banks, ? Hospitals, Factories, Ships, etc.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\" style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: center; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>EVACUATION SIGNS</span></strong> <span>\n<br style=\"mso-special-character: line-break;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]-->\n<br style=\"mso-special-character: line-break;\">\n<!--[endif]--></span>\n</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\" style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: center; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%284%29.png\"><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%285%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p align=\"center\" style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: center; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%286%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.10 Fire propagation</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>ELECTRIC MOTORS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>They are a common cause of fires on board. ? If this does not adequately maintained or when working beyond their capacity or useful life, is very likely to cause problems. ? Electric motors require regular inspection, testing, cleaning and lubrication. ? If one of the windings is shorted or has a ground wire can make the engine bearing is overheating. ? In any case, it is an ignition source, which in the circumstances may be the source of a serious fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>ELECTRICAL</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Electrical equipment permanently installed is subject to a lot of wear due to saline air causes corrosion and vibration of the ship breaks the isolation and some pieces of equipment, which results in the electrical equipment or conductors can produce sparks that are excellent sources of ignition if flammable gases in the vicinity.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>The electrical equipment installed on ships should be of special design, provide maintenance and should only be used for the purpose for which it has been placed. The person is not suitable or no knowledge of how to use it should help.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>Lockers AND DEPOSITS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>When you are on board the ship, and for reasons of haste or struggling to get the supplies are kept in lockers and balances \"provisional\" way, hoping for a later time the proper stowage of such provisions, and there are times that later time never comes and the ship sets sail with their belongings and badly stowed provisions. ? When you begin nodding and balances objects fall, hit, mixed, sometimes breaking electrical circuits or foci, and even give pipes where leaks occur. All this means that fire starts, due to the nature of most of the things that are stored in the lockers of ships, is very easy to reach serious proportions.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>The best way to avoid this is to stow and store things away.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>KITCHEN</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>The kitchen and its dependencies in most of the ships are very busy places, there is usually a lot of activity, there are several people working on a not very large, there is less abundant flame or heat build-up of fat and other combustible materials. ? The kitchen is considered dangerous place.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>BILGE AREAS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>First bilges are the lower parts of the hold of a ship, comprising the space between the floors where all the waters that for whatever reason have no direct access to the sea accumulate. ? Fires in the bilges are caused by excessive accumulation of petroleum products from piping leaks and losses. ? These products vaporize easily and gases accumulate in the bilge space where they mix with atmospheric oxygen. ? From this point, a bad match off, lit a cigarette carelessly thrown a spark either will produce a fire. ? Bilge fires spread very easily and are not easy to control. ? As a precaution careful inspection of the bilges and often recommended. ? If too much oil or fuel is observed that there is some means to locate and repair leaks.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.11 Smoke and fire detection</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Sensors or fire detectors can be of the following types:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Photoelectric Smoke Detectors</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>These sensors are useful for small fires, which provides for the start of the fire with lots of smoke, as when burning organic material.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>These sensors detect quickly, allowing immediate action with hand-fighting equipment and mastering the flames before they could spread.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>It works by Tyndall effect, based on light scattering.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>It is also used in fires caused by electricity.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Detectors or ? Smoke mechanics</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>These detectors operate by an ionization chamber.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>The detector has a radioactive source of Americium 241 (Am-241) with less than or equal to 1 micro curie activity; ? has two cameras: one for measuring and other compensation. ? Usually mounted in locations where the occurrence of fire is expected smoky.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>You can detect invisible particles of combustion and visible smoke; ? quickly detects black smoke.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>This is a very rarely used for its radioactive source and detector maintenance expensive.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Fixed Thermo Temperature Detectors</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Detects when the temperature rises flames effect.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>At a given temperature the detector goes off and sends the signal to the central fire detection.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Mounts in any venue where high temperature rise in case of fire is expected.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Detector Thermo Detectors</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>They work by detecting two different ways:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; mso-add-space: auto; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo8;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Fixed temperature.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; mso-add-space: auto; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-list: l8 level1 lfo8;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Temperature gradient. ? When the temperature is increased gradually; ? the gradient according to the ma</span><span>nufacturer, usually between 5 ? </span><span>C / minute to 10 / minute.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>These detectors (as heat) are mounted in areas or places where it is predicted that may arise unprovoked fire smoke to avoid false alarms.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>They are installed where fast temperature rises are expected in case of fire, caused for example by liquid fuels.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Flame Detectors</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Can be:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; mso-add-space: auto; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo9;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Infrared (IR) are mounted to detect fires is expected to start flame infrared radiation.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; mso-add-space: auto; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo9;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>U</span><span>ltraviolet flame (IV): are mounted expected when the flame is of ultraviolet radiation, as in the case of fuel gases such as butane, methane, etc.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; mso-add-space: auto; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-list: l1 level1 lfo9;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>U</span><span>ltraviolet and infrared (IR + IV) are mounted for detecting two types of radiation.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Linear Infrared or Infrared Barriers</span></strong><span> ? (smoke detected).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>These detectors detect smoke and are used in cases of large volumes and heights over 12 meters. ? Have a distance of between 10 and 100 m.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>They consist of two parts: transmitter and receiver, located opposite each other, perfectly aligned to avoid false alarms. ? Crossing the infrared beam they emit, detect and give warning.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Cable Linear Temperature Detector</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>This cable line sensor is activated by temperature and melts when heated produces an insulating layer and a short circuit that outputs the signal to the PBX.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>It is used in cable tunnels, riding zigzag on cable trays and storage tanks for liquid fuels with conical roof.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Repeaters panels</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Repeaters panels are installed to signal the fire alarm in another place where the central fire detection is.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>This team consists of a cadre of lads, one for each detection zone.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Automatic fire detection control is mounted in a location where surveillance; ? repeater panel is usually located in the maintenance area.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>There are two different systems, namely:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; mso-add-space: auto; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Conventional detection system.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>This system can identify its elements by groups or detection zones, also called ? <span style=\"mso-bidi-font-style: italic;\">collective identification</span>. When one of them is excited by starting a fire, transmit an alarm to the central detector with accurate identification of the area.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; mso-add-space: auto; text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-list: l4 level1 lfo7;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Microprocessor analogy-detection system for identifying individual elements.</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>This system allows individual identification of each detector element.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Automatic detection centre has a CPU, a keyboard controller with buttons and LEDs, a display screen where the sensing element that alerts, electronic control cards detection loops, a power supply of 220 VLTs is identified with amperage needed to power the elements of the system.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>This system also has other different elements to the conventional system, namely:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Monitor Module detection zones to conventional detectors.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Monitoring modules for technical signals.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Control modules and activation (solenoids, audible alarms, electromagnetic retainers, etc.)</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Small modules for monitoring alarm buttons.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Manual Call</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>These buttons are mounted with the sensing system. ? It is important to differentiate the signal emitted by the detector, since when transmitting the signal is initiated by being activated by a person. ? This alarm is given priority over the detector and indicating presence of flames and acts before the detector perceives it.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>The alarm buttons are mounted so that they are visible, marked and should not be wider than 25 m., Preferably located near the stairwells, evacuation corridors, emergency exits, exit doors, plants etc.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><strong><span>Acoustic Alarms </span></strong><span>(sirens, acoustic, bells, etc.)</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Audible alarms warn persons within the local area plant of the start of a fire, so they can quickly evacuate; ? it is therefore necessary that it can be heard anywhere.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>If the area in question cannot hear well the alarm, the activities that take place or for other conditions where there is loud noise emission, optical-acoustic systems are mounted with an intermittent flash to be displayed immediately.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.12 Automatic fire alarm</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>HOW TO DETECT A FIRE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Automatic fire detection can discover the fire in its early stage, thereby contributing decisively to reduce damage that could occur.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Different types of sensors detect incipient fire through fire phenomena, such as smoke, flame, heat, etc.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>It is a device that produces an alarm when it detects a fire in the protected area. ? The alarm is designed to alert those responsible for ship safety, including personnel on duty, and to alert the crew.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>It is vital to discover the fire as soon as possible, as it is much easier to confine, control and extinguish it before it has reached large proportions. ? Therefore, a good fire detection system, well known and understood by the crew maintained, is the best element for fire protection on board.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: normal; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%287%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Detection systems on board are willing to produce, in case of fire, a visible and audible alarm that is perceived on the bridge and in the engine room. ? The device receiving the alarm will indicate the existence and location of the fire on board. ? This equipment is usually located on the bridge and sometimes the fourth C02.In the engine room there is usually only a buzzer or bell. ? Perceiving a fire alarm, the officer of the watch on the bridge will activate the general alarm fire crew to occupy their posts and fire functions as set forth in the rules fire on board. ? In all cases, immediately notify the Captain who will investigate the cause of the alarm, and if this is true proceed to confinement, control and extinguishment under the procedure established under the direction of Captain. ? If it is a false alarm, it is necessary to investigate the cause and correct. ? In any case, you should verify the correct operation of the fire detection and alarm system and get it back in working order.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>For fire detection, different systems among which we can mention:</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>AUTOMATED SYSTEMS FIRE DETECTION</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Fire detectors and alarms.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Emergency power energy supply.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Normal source to the power supply.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Control fire detection unit.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>DETECTORS AND FIRE ALARMS</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>These detect the presence of heat, smoke, flames or other indications of fire and produce a corresponding signal which is received by the alarm generator equipment. ? The visible alarm consists of one or more red lights on the control units and positions within the vessel. ? Audible alarms consist of bells or fire. Usually generally bell ringers which have a diameter between 15 and 20 cm. ? When a fire is detected, the alarm visible (red) remains illuminated and audible alarms (bells) remain sound until acted upon a muffler device doorbells that do not turn off the red light. ? This light only goes by the manual system reset.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Modern alarms systems allow concurrently receive fire alarms from various points without any interference between each other.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Failures in providing supply to the fire detection and alarm power advertised by the sound of an alarm, in which case a light signal appears. ? The malfunction of the system, such as rupture or disconnection of one or more electrical cables warning detection system because blue light is on and the same alarm sounds in the event of power failure. ? There are certain teams that register malfunctions and fire alarms.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>2.13 Classification of fires and appropriate extinguishing agents</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>FIRES</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>From the point of view of fire so that they can be externalized classified into two groups in turn:</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>FLAMES</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>The flames are difficult to define, as there are various combustion processes, there are different types of flames.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>We can say without equivocation that basically called, is the product of a rapid and persistent chemical reaction that gives off heat and light. ? This reaction must stand on its own, if not extinct, until the concentration of the fuel or oxidizer, not fall below the minimum necessary to keep (this definition, which we all know).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Also remember that not all processes of \"burning\" or \"decomposition\" produce flames, but develop heat.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Flames hold different colours and are almost invisible (to the human eye), these circumstances, which are directly related with fuels and oxidizers which react as well to the temperature generated by this reaction.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>As we know, it is a visible manifestation of fire and is the result of heating to incandescence of small particles of metals or composite elements (especially incomplete combustion).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>A<span class=\"apple-converted-space\"> ? </span><strong>fire alarm system</strong>is a set of electric/electronic devices/equipment working together to detect and alert people through visual and audio appliances when smoke/fire is present. These alarms may be activated from smoke detectors, heat detectors, water flow sensors, which are automatic or from a manual fire alarm pull station.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>SURFACE OR WITHOUT FIRE FLAME</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>As the name implies, the combustion does not occur in space but strictly a surface oxidation occurs. ? Such fires also called coal surface in red, glow, glowing embers, etc. ? Its main feature is the absence of flame. ? The reaction kinetics is low and the combustion surface and develops toward the core of burning material.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>FIRES FLAME</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>They are clear examples of this type of fire combustion gases or vapours ? of flammable liquids that may or may not light. ? Arden simultaneously throughout their mass. ? Given the high combustion rate that characterizes the extinction must be swift and decisive.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 18.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%288%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>CLASSIFICATION OF FIRES AND APPLICABLE EXTINGUISHING AGENTES </span></strong>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Are varied extinguishing agents used in laptops, so, it also changed the degree of effectiveness of each of them and the limitations in their applicability. ? They can be classified into:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%289%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>WATER</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>For its abundance is the most common in fire fighting operations. ? Your Extinguisher action is based on cooling the burning material. ? Is applied in the form of a pressurized jet, or as a fine spray over the entire burning surface. ? It is well, thrown in large quantities through hoses or sprinklers on relatively small amounts of oil.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2810%29.png\"><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2811%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>This extinguishing agent can only be applied in the Class \"A\" fires, and in some cases fires CLASS \"B\" as the oil fires, which should be applied in the manner described. ? Never use in fires CLASS \"C\", and even less in the Class \"D\" fires.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<strong><span>FOAM</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The foam may be produced by mixing a solution of aluminium sulphate with other sodium bicarbonate and adding a stabilizer. ? In laptops foam is produced by the reaction of the two mentioned solutions, resulting in an internal pressure resulting from carbon dioxide generation, capable of impelling the foam to a distance of seven (7) feet.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Foam remove oxygen to form a layer which prevents the passage of air, and also cools somewhat. ? Is applied as a layer covering the surface of the burning liquid. ? However, in many cases, the fumes arising from burning substances pass through the foam layer, and if the concentration is sufficient, it will burn up.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n</a><!-- [if mso & !supportInlineShapes & supportFields]><span lang=EN-US><span style=\'mso-spacerun:yes\'> ? </span>SHAPE \\* MERGEFORMAT <![endif]--> <!-- [if mso & !supportInlineShapes & supportFields]><span lang=EN-US><v:shape id=\"_x0000_i1025\" type=\"#_x0000_t75\" style=\'width:.85pt; height:.85pt\'> <v:imagedata croptop=\"-65520f\" cropbottom=\"65520f\"/> </v:shape><![endif]-->\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\nv<img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2812%29.png\">\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>It is especially useful in extinguishing class B fires, and where the smothering effect of extinguishing agent is of great importance. ? Certain solvents (alcohols, acetone, etc.) rolled foam, which is not convenient to use it on fire of these substances. ? Being electrically conductive should never be used on Class C fires also is contraindicated in Class D. Fires</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>CARBON DIOXIDE CO2</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>This gas can be stored under pressure in portable extinguishers and discharged through a special nozzle on the site where it is needed. ? The Extinguisher characteristic of carbon dioxide is its smothering effect accompanied by a slight cooling. ? Do not use in confined or poorly ventilated areas, as the user can be subject to mechanical asphyxia due to lack of oxygen, it is suitable for Class B fires and Class C. Not suitable for other types of fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2813%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>DRY CHEMICAL</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The products are mixtures of finely pulverized combustible, such as Sodium Carbonate, Sodium Bicarbonate, Sodium Sulphate, Sodium Silicate, Bentonite, etc. ? Works by drowning as it applies attempting to form a layer on the burning material.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>In laptops the extinguishing agent is ejected by the pressure produced by a nitrogen capsule, located inside the fire extinguisher and which is broken at the time of use.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 35.0pt;\">\n</a><!-- [if mso & !supportInlineShapes & supportFields]><span lang=EN-US><span style=\'mso-spacerun:yes\'> ? </span>SHAPE \\* MERGEFORMAT <![endif]--> <!-- [if mso & !supportInlineShapes & supportFields]><span lang=EN-US><v:shape id=\"_x0000_i1025\" type=\"#_x0000_t75\" style=\'width:.85pt; height:.85pt\'> <v:imagedata croptop=\"-65520f\" cropbottom=\"65520f\"/> </v:shape><![endif]-->\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 0cm 0cm .0001pt 35.0pt;\">\n<img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%2814%29.png\">\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>According to the composition of the mixture, the dry chemical is suitable for Class A, B fires, and C. No e suitable for Class D fires.</span>\n</p>',NULL,'text',NULL,32767,0,'2026-04-19 03:45:28',1),(393,152,'3. Selection of firefighting devices and equipment','<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 150%;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>Fire nozzles and hoses</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>FIRE HOSE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>One of the most important units in fire fighting hose is. ? Ideally, that will operate efficiently the team for handling a fire hose shall consist of four members. ? It is recommended that at least two persons handling hose 38 mm (1/2 inch) and three hose 64 mm (2 1/2 inches).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The key man in the team handling the hose is the man who operates the nozzle, it is in who controls the jet type and address. ? In many cases the man must take decisions before the top can point them out.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The man who runs the nozzle should have the training and discipline to ensure that all persons who handle the hose will efficiently moving towards the fire and the water is directed to the appropriate site of the fire. ? This is a responsible position and should be assigned to a member of the crew or has received special training in fire fighting. ? The man who runs the nozzle must also be a man who is thoroughly familiar with the ship and all its elements.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Immediately behind the man who drives the hose is supportive man. ? This<span style=\"mso-spacerun: yes;\"> ? </span>man carries the weight of the hose and absorbs some of the reaction of the jet outlet, so that the man who runs the nozzle has to do much effort. ? To maintain their position, both the man and the man supporting the nozzle must act in perfect coordination. The other team members are handling the hose is positioned along the same manoeuvre to assist in forward or reverse.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Staff may be appropriate to use machines to handle the hoses that are assigned to the protection of machinery spaces, as they know the machine and are familiar with that part of the ship. ? Additionally, these crews have probably been in the vicinity of the fire when it has broken and can know. ? For the same reason, the components of the equipment hose should be those who often work in the closest place to where the fire points.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>FORWARD WITH HOSE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>When there has been a fire alarm, the hose must be driven as close to the fire of the valve mouth (hydrant) is opened. ? Without water, the hose is very light and easy to handle. ? It may be advanced and be comfortable with it and quickly to the right place. ? Once the hose water pressure has taken heavy and unwieldy. ? The members of the gang who run the hose become tired of having to move the weight! ? Additional water and especially if they have to go up or down stairs or hose along passageways. ? If you also take an apparatus for artificial respiration, effort does consume oxygen or air much faster than the normal situation.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The hose should be handled as follows:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Man of the nozzle and the support holding the first section of hose and advance towards the fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The third team member holds the hose midsection and moves with it.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The fourth member of the team remains in the hose checkpoint to open the fire hydrant and the above helps.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>When the man of the nozzle is in position, ask open water.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>When the water has filled the hose, the third and fourth member of the team hold hoses and equipment must withstand any swing, while verifying the links to see if there are water spills or leaks.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>If so, they can squeeze more links with the corresponding key.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>After the water has arrived, the man of the nozzle must open slightly to allow the release of air that is contained within the hose.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The hose should be closed at the moment when water starts to flow thus leaving the hose ready for self.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>During fire drills all hoses should be deployed and installed as if you were fighting a real fire. ? The training must be as realistic as practical and possible. ? The groups must handle the actual practice hoses leading to decks or higher, pass them through brokers via stairs and points some difficulty.</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>PROPER USE OF JET PRODUCED BY HOSE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The way that the jets must be applied depends on the fire situation. ? The man who runs the nozzle must know the type of jet you have to use and when to use it according to the conditions of the fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 150%;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>3.2<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1;\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span>Mobile devices</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Cell foam generating equipment, especially suitable for fire fighting liquid fuels. ? Its use in fire protection is widely recommended wherever flammable liquids present a serious fire hazard and where a quick response is needed to extinguish. ? It is also suitable for reservoirs paper, cardboard and textile companies.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Frothing tank: ? \n<br>\nMade of plastic resin reinforced with fibre glass (FRP). ? The glass fibre reinforcement provides the overall chemical resistance and excellent dielectric behaviour of harsh chemicals that compose the foam agents. Poses concentrated at the top a loading of frothing with polypropylene cap, threaded 1 ? ? \"W. Gas and a second suction inlet mouth frothing spout. ? At the bottom is a spout with screw cap 1 \"Gas polypropylene. ? \n<br style=\"mso-special-character: line-break;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]-->\n<br style=\"mso-special-character: line-break;\">\n<!--[endif]--></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Chassis: The unit is mounted on the chassis of steel pipe ? \"steel shaft and 400 mm diameter wheels with steel rims and solid rubber. ? At the bottom bears two support legs, one fixed and one mobile, and at the top, carrying handle. ? The chassis assembly is painted with nail baked black. ? \n<br>\nDispenser foam: keeps a dispenser alloy bronze model DL-6, it should be mounted on the top of the tank and lead added the following components: ? </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo11; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">a.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>A ball valve of a) ? \"that regulates the passage of emulsifier whose maximum dose is scheduled at 6% ? </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo11; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">b.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>A suction hose ? \"at the end of which is immersed fisher tube in the tank.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l0 level1 lfo11; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">c.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>The inlet and outlet of the dispenser can be 2 ? \"2\", 1 ? \"or 1 ? \" female swivel (input) and fixed male (output).</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>\n<br>\nThrows Foam: Made of aluminium alloy body and brass union. ? Resistant ? the rough treatment, harsh chemicals, etc. ? It is suitable for generating 1000 litres / min. ? Foam with an expansion. 1: 6-7 Kg / cm2 ? \n<br>\nManga Foam: in synthetic fibre, resistant to aggressive chemicals of Foaming agents can be 2 ? \"2\", 1 ? \"or 1 ? \" x 15/20 or 25 meters long. ? With man drill s Glands and bronze at the ends. ? \n<br style=\"mso-special-character: line-break;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLineBreakNewLine]-->\n<br style=\"mso-special-character: line-break;\">\n<!--[endif]--></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Main Features: ? </span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Pressure: The proper pressure for optimum operation of the equipment is 7 kg / cm2. ? But it works ? from 2.5 Kg / cm2.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Weight of empty equipment. 55 Kg.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Weight loaded team. ? 175 Kg.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>\n<br>\n<strong>OPERATING INSTRUCTIONS:</strong> ? </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo10; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Connect the water supply hose to the inlet of the dispenser.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo10; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Extend the hose and lance with foam. ? </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo10; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Open ball valve ? \"to allow the passage of emulsifier. ? </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l5 level1 lfo10; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Direct Foam jet towards the base of the fire to extinguish.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>\n<br>\n<strong>INSTRUCTIONS AFTER OPERATION:</strong> ? </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l24 level1 lfo12; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">1.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Disconnect the hose from the machine and wash it with clean water background removing any remaining frothing and return to stay at bay. ? </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l24 level1 lfo12; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">2.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Wash the dispenser and the fisher tube. ? </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l24 level1 lfo12; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">3.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>If you use only part complete the frothing tank contents.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l24 level1 lfo12; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">4.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>If all the frothing is used internally wash the tank with clean water and dry removing the inner stopper, and fill up again close to 120 Lts. ? of frothing. ? </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l24 level1 lfo12; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">5.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Rinse thoroughly with clean water and spear stowed in its housing. ? </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"mso-add-space: auto; text-indent: -18.0pt; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1; mso-list: l24 level1 lfo12; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 36.0pt;\">\n<!-- [if !supportLists]--><span><span style=\"mso-list: Ignore;\">6.<span style=\"font: 7.0pt \'Times New Roman\';\"> ? ? ? ? </span></span><!--[endif]--><span>Never leave dirty equipment after use.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 150%;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>3.3<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1;\"> ? ? ? ? </span>Portable fire extinguishers</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>There are different kinds of portable fire extinguishers that we often see in the bulkheads of ships on the walls of buildings and other points. ? You can learn about the characteristics of the fire by classification. ? Classification of fire extinguishers does not always coincide. ? Generally, extinguishers are classified in capital letters; ? Arabic numerals and Roman numerals.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Roman numerals can also appear extinguishers and give an indication of the size of the extinguisher. ? They range from 1 for the smallest to the largest corresponding V.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>General rules for the use of portable fire fighting equipment just to discover a fire:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Giving voice \"Fire ...\" giving meaningful data on the location of fire</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Activate the nearest fire alarm.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Giving voice calling for help, in a calm and quiet, and especially clear.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>If fire, according to the first to detect it, is of small proportion can be attempted attack with portable extinguisher, bearing in mind that:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Never cross a fire to fetch a fire extinguisher if there is the slightest suspicion of being able to get caught by the scrub.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>To extinguish the fire if you have to go into a behavior, it is necessary to always have without exception an escape route by fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>If the attempt to extinguish the fire with the extinguisher fails, you must immediately leave the burning chamber and close the door. ? Who detected the fire must remain in place to provide information to fire personnel who will come.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 150%;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>3.4<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1;\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span>Firefighter team</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>In accordance with the provisions of SOLAS 1974 (1981 Amendments) fire fighter equipment include:</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>A personal computer comprising:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Protective clothing of material to protect the skin from the heat is waterproof.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Boots and rubber gloves that are not electro conductors.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>A rigid hull.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>An electric safety lamp with a minimum burning period of three hours.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>A respiratory system that could be adopted:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>An antismoke helmet or smoke mask anti provided with a suitable air pump adequate air and a rather long flexible especially for air either.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>A self-contained breathing apparatus operated by compressed air cylinders which have a capacity of 1,200 litres of air at least or other self-contained breathing apparatus that can run for 30 minutes for the same.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>There will be board enough spare charges for the use of equipment.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Each respiratory keep a fireproof lifeline of sufficient length and strength to be bound by or snap hook to the harness of breathing apparatus or to a separate belt. All vessels shall carry at least two fire crew gears.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>The fireman\'s outfits are stored ready for immediate use in easily accessible sites, if more than one fire equipment is carried; they shall be stored in widely separated positions together.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 150%;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>3.5<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1;\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span>Fire blankets</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>A fire sheet or fire </span><span>blanket is a security devise designed to extinguish small incipient fires. ? It consists of a sheet of material ? flame retardant</span><span>that <span style=\"color: black;\">is placed over the fire to smother it, to prevent the arrival of oxygen.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%281%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Fire blanket: demonstration of its use to extinguish a fire declared.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Small, such as used in kitchen and domestic environments, fire blankets are generally made ? ? </span><span>of ? </span><a title=\"Fiberglass\" href=\"http://translate.google.com/translate?hl=es&amp;prev=_t&amp;sl=es&amp;tl=en&amp;u=http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fibra_de_vidrio\"><span>fiber glass</span></a></span><span>, and are folded in a sheath or bag quick release, to facilitate storage. ? Blankets ? </span><a title=\"Nomex\" href=\"http://translate.google.com/translate?hl=es&amp;prev=_t&amp;sl=es&amp;tl=en&amp;u=http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nomex\"><span>Nomex</span></a></span><span>and have a look similar to a conventional blanket texture, but are made ? ? of fire retardant fibres and are safe for the skin, eyes and respiratory tract.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%282%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Demonstration of a fire blanket to smother a fire caused.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>These blankets larger, for use in situations ? </span><a title=\"Laboratory\" href=\"http://translate.google.com/translate?hl=es&amp;prev=_t&amp;sl=es&amp;tl=en&amp;u=http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Laboratorio\"><span>laboratory</span></a></span><span>and industry, often are made ? ? of ? </span><a title=\"Wool\" href=\"http://translate.google.com/translate?hl=es&amp;prev=_t&amp;sl=es&amp;tl=en&amp;u=http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lana\"><span>wool</span></a></span><span>(sometimes treated with a liquid flame retardant). ? These blankets are usually mounted in vertical quick-release cabinets so they can be easily removed and can be wrapped around a person whose clothing is on fire. ? </span><a href=\"http://translate.google.com/translate?hl=es&amp;prev=_t&amp;sl=es&amp;tl=en&amp;u=http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manta_ign%25C3%25ADfuga%23cite_note-3#cite_note-3\"><sup><span>3</span></sup></a></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>There are special blankets for use by</span><span>professional welder that support up to 1600 ? C. ? (ANSI FM-<span style=\"color: black;\">4950)</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 150%;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>3.6<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1;\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span>Knowledge of fire safety provisions</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Its mission is technical advice, campaigns and tasks relating to fire prevention groups and organizations and to ensure the application in the scope of the Fire and Rescue Service of all existing legislation in the field of fire prevention and will write reports on each case as requested.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>It performs the necessary theoretical and practical training for all staff are in the best conditions for performing their tasks.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 150%;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>3.7<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1;\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span>Fire alarms and first measures</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 18.0pt; mso-outline-level: 2; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><span>Ionization Detectors</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Ionization detectors are the most common types of smoke detectors and fire alarms. ? Ionization detectors create a harmless level of ionizing radiation by using an electrical current to create and free positively charged ions in the air. ? Smoke from a fire interferes with the constant electric current created by ionization. ? When electric current is inhibited, the alarm sounds to indicate the presence of smoke or fire. ? Ionization detectors may be powered by small batteries which must be periodically checked and replaced. ? Ionization detectors are often the most reliable smoke detectors available for regular consumers, though not always respond to small fires or smouldering fires.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 18.0pt; mso-outline-level: 2; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><span>Photoelectric</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Photoelectric detectors and alarms using light sensitive technology to detect the presence of smoke in a room. ? In photoelectric detectors, a beam of light is emitted from the detector in a straight line. ? A photoelectric sensor is disposed at an angle with the light beam so that it can detect the degree of refraction of the beam. ? When smoke passes the light beam, the light particles scatter and refract throughout the room. ? The ? light sensor</span><span>detects and sounds an alarm refraction. ? Photoelectric detectors respond better to small fires and smoke than larger fires.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 18.0pt; mso-outline-level: 2; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<strong><span>Thermal Detectors</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Thermal or heat detectors are not a common type of fire alarm, and do not react to smoke. ? Instead, heat detectors are designed to respond once the temperature exceeds a set limit. ? Heat detectors are typically not suitable for domestic use because it does not respond until a fire has raised the temperature ? sufficient</span><span>to trigger the alarm. ? In contrast, thermal detectors are used in areas where smoke is constantly present, and industrial buildings or commercial kitchens.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 150%;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>3.8<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1;\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span>Firefighting</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>It\'s called fire protection to all available measures in buildings to protect them from the action of ? </span><a title=\"Fire\" href=\"http://translate.google.com/translate?hl=es&amp;prev=_t&amp;sl=es&amp;tl=en&amp;u=http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fuego\"><span>fire</span></a></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Generally, it comes with them to get three purposes:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Saving Lives</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Minimize economic losses caused by fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Getting construction activities to be resumed in the shortest possible time.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Saving human lives is usually the sole purpose of the rules of the various states and the other two insurers impose lowering of the more appropriate policies are the means.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 5.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>The basic fire safety measures can be classified into two types:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Passive measures: These measures affecting the ? </span><a title=\"Project\" href=\"http://translate.google.com/translate?hl=es&amp;prev=_t&amp;sl=es&amp;tl=en&amp;u=http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Proyecto\"><span>project</span></a></span><span> ? or the ? </span><a title=\"Construction\" href=\"http://translate.google.com/translate?hl=es&amp;prev=_t&amp;sl=es&amp;tl=en&amp;u=http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Construcci%25C3%25B3n\"><span>construction</span></a></span><span> ? of the building, first facilitating the evacuation of users present in case of fire through roads ( ? </span><a title=\"Hall (architecture)\" href=\"http://translate.google.com/translate?hl=es&amp;prev=_t&amp;sl=es&amp;tl=en&amp;u=http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pasillo_%2528arquitectura%2529\"><span>corridors</span></a></span><span> ? and ? </span><a title=\"Ladder\" href=\"http://translate.google.com/translate?hl=es&amp;prev=_t&amp;sl=es&amp;tl=en&amp;u=http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escalera\"><span>stairs</span></a></span><span> ? ) of sufficient size and secondly slowing and confining the action of the fire so that it does not spread very quickly or stand before invading other areas.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Active Measures: Basically manifest in fire fighting facilities</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 150%;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>3.9<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1;\"> ? ? ? ? ? ? </span>Means to fight fires</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>An immediate response is, as its name indicates, a response that has to be undertakenpromptly when a fire occurs to prevent its spreading and intensity. Some shipboard fires become a disaster because they have not been properly responded to at their incipientstage (Lygate, 1998). For the purpose of this paper, this Chapter deals with the response that takes place from the time a fire is discovered or detected by any equipment until the captain has the command and control of the situation. My aim is to analyses the response on the discovery of the fire, both the crew ? s response and that of the officer of watch. However, it is not under the scope of this dissertation to cover the response that is carried out automatically by automatic devices on board some particular ships.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>On the discovery of a fire or unexpected strange smoke, a crew member is aware of the possible danger. If he cannot find the flames or the source of the smoke, he should first attempt to locate the fire, and then alert the whole ship by raising the alarm, attempt to fight the fire and finally report the situation to the officer of watch. This procedure can be adopted on board to give a very first response to fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Locating the fire</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Locating the fire is of extreme importance because if successfully achieved, it will enable the crew member to assess the situation and conduct his response. The fire can be discovered by the crew member or detected by special equipment. Early detection or discovery provides quick information concerning, not only the place of the fire but also, possibly, its nature. This will enable the selection of an adequate and feasible response. The crew member has various methods and pieces of equipment at his disposal for this purpose. </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Early detection </span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Early detection is of prime importance. In the case of the two fires on board Scandinavian Star, the one that caused the disaster could have been located fifteen minutes after the start of the fire As a result; the fire could be extinguished in time. In the author ? s opinion, if the attempt to discover the location of a hidden fire is likely to be unsuccessful, the alarm should be raised immediately.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Location of the fire</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The location of the fire is very important, as it dictates not only the access to the fire for the fighting operation but also the way to escape in case it has to take place. For example, a fire on the main deck is probably less dangerous, considering the accessibility, than one in a cargo hold. The location of the fire also determines the appropriate extinguishing agent to fight it.For example, a cargo hold fire is fought with CO2 while a fire in the accommodation, unless involving electricity, is usually fought with water. Furthermore, knowing the location of the fire helps to identify what can be threatened or persons in danger if the fire should spread.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Nature of the fire</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>The nature of the fire determines the fighting agent that is to be used to extinguish the fire. Cooling the burnt material below its ignition temperature fights fires in bedding, or clothing, known as class A fires. Meanwhile, fire in flammable liquids, known as class B fire, is dealt with by cutting off the supply of air. Class A fires are mostly fought by water while class B fires are fought by the use of water fog, foam, carbon dioxide or other gases such as halotrone and argonit, and dry chemicals. Therefore, ignorance and misjudging of the nature of the fire could easily promote its spreading by undertaking inappropriate action.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Methods</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Several methods can be used to locate the fire. One method is to touch a compartment door or a bulkhead with a bare hand to check for temperature. If it is hot, it means that it is heated by a fire from behind. The presence of discolored or blistering paint found on a bulkhead may also indicate that fire is present behind it. In addition, if smoke can be observed passing through wiring holes or other routes such as portholes or zipping under a door, it means that fire is present behind. Furthermore, if unusual noise can be heard, it is a possible indication that fire may be present.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Precautions</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Some precautions should be considered when trying to find the location of a fire. If there is a suspicion that a fire may be hidden behind a compartment door, it is imperative not to open the door during the first response to prevent explosion due to demand of O2 of the fire. The person has to be equipped with charged hose line at hand, which is not to be performed during this immediate response. In addition, it is vital that the crew member does not persist in trying to find the location of the fire if it is likely to put his life in jeopardy.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Equipment</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Many pieces of equipment can be used for automatic detection of fire as it is described in Consolidated edition 1997 of SOLAS International Convention for the Safety of Life at Sea, 1974) such as heat and smoke detectors. There are also three other type of detectors, namely, the flame detector, heart and differential detector. The flame detector is suitable for installation in place where flames can develop rapidly in case of fire. The differential detector is activated when there is abnormal rise of temperature for example 3 ? C in 20 seconds ( Bo O, 1987). The smoke detector is the most efficient among those automatic detectors considering the response time but, in the author ? s opinion, it is desirable to use a combination of them, if possible, to assist in case one fails when needed.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 150%;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>3.10<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1;\"> ? ? ? ? </span>Procedures for fighting fires</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>When there is a fire on board of a vessel, it is important to follow protocol and respond to the fire as soon as possible. Fire on board of a vessel is one of the most dangerous emergencies on the water. That is why you should always be prepared.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Fire on ships mostly starts in the engine room. They can be caused by, for example, oil leakages, incidents with the boiler, or electrical failures.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: normal;\">\n<span>Once a fire is detected, there are several actions to take to ensure safety of the ship and its crew members:</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The general alarm should be sounded.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The bridge team has to be informed.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>The fire party must come together.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Try to isolate the fire by closing ventilation system, skylights, doors, etc.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Before entering the fire space, crew should wear the appropriate PPE and use the proper fire extinguishing system, regarding the type of fire.</span>\n</p>\n\n</span><!--[endif]--><span>Interested parties have to be notified.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify;\">\n<span>A fire on a ship is a hazard for firefighters because of the way vessels are constructed. The access to the ship is often difficult. The possibility of fire spreading makes it hard to contain it. Therefore, in order to contain a fire as soon as possible, it is vital that firefighters have access to the necessary equipment. In addition to that, clear way finding on the ship is very important as well.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify;\">\n<span>Low Location Lighting</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span>A fire on board of a ship can fill the corridors and rooms up with smoke. This makes way finding difficult and causes disorientation. ? </span><a href=\"https://t-iss.com/catalogue/?categories=8\"><span>A Low Location Lighting (LLL) system</span></a></span><span> ? is designed to direct people to safety. The LLL is made of photoluminiscent products to highlight the rooms and corridors. This can be life-saving during a fire on a vessel. The illuminated LLL also work during a power failure.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%283%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline; margin: 12.0pt 0cm .0001pt 0cm;\">\n<span>Electrical Low Location Lighting</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<a href=\"https://t-iss.com/catalogue/product/low-location-light-trail-dots/?categories=8\"><span>Electrical Low Location Lighting systems</span></a></span><span> ? are often chosen for a more luxurious look. Electrical LLL systems can be installed both floor-mounted and wall-mounted. They are considered ? active ? systems, compared to ? passive ? LLL systems made of photo luminescent material.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span>Low Location Lighting Products and Installation</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; tab-stops: 53.75pt; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<a href=\"https://t-iss.com/catalogue/?categories=8\"><span>Here you can find</span></a></span><span> ? a comprehensive overview of the LLL products we offer. Next to our Low Location Lighting products we offer several engineering services; from LLL installation to required periodic LLL measurements. For more information about Low Location Lighting implementation and certification services, please visit our Low Location Lighting services page on our website.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; tab-stops: 53.75pt; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span>Escape Signs Help When There ? s A Fire On Board</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span>Safety Signs are crucial in every work environment. They can make visitors and crew members aware of possible dangers. In case of a fire on board, ? </span><a href=\"https://t-iss.com/catalogue/product/escape-route-signs/?categories=7\"><span>escape signs</span></a></span><span> ? and escape routes can safe lives. The Escape signs provide a clear, safe way to evacuate the corridors and rooms, guiding crew members to an assembly point.At T-ISS we offer environmentally friendly, back-printed photo luminescent PETX signage products. ? The Escape Signs from Safe Sign are PVC-free and recyclable, and there for better for the environment. The photo luminescent signs make sure that the signs are visible even during a power failure.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span>IMO firefighting signs</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span> ? Under the name of ? </span><a href=\"https://safesign.info/\"><span>Safe Sign</span></a></span><span> ? we also offer ? </span><a href=\"https://t-iss.com/catalogue/product/fire-fighting-signs/?categories=7\"><span>Fire Fighting Signs</span></a></span><span>. These signs, specifically designed for fire emergencies, are placed near firefighting equipment on board. This makes it easier for crew members to find the needed equipment in case of a fire. These red firefighting signs have the same photo luminescent abilities, making them more visible.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span>The Fire Fighting Signs are corresponding to IMO Res. A760 (18) as amended ISO 17631:2002.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%284%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span>Fire Plan Container</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span>A ? </span><a href=\"https://t-iss.com/catalogue/product/fire-plan-container-/?categories=4\"><span>fire plan container</span></a></span><span> ? is designed to contain a fire plan for emergency situations. It stores a copy of a fire plan or escape plan. Fire &amp; Safety plans must be always exhibited for the guidance of the ship ? s crews showing clearly for each deck as required by the SOLAS 1974 Reg. The Fire plan container by T-ISS is a watertight container designed for both offshore and marine.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span>The red coated fire plan container is made of stainless steel. The fire plan container is clearly visible because of the photo luminescent ? fire plan ? label. It is suitable for all standard fire plan sizes.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span>International Shore Connection</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin: 0cm; margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; background: white; vertical-align: baseline;\">\n<span>The goal of the ? </span><a href=\"https://t-iss.com/catalogue/product/international-shore-connection/?categories=4\"><span>International shore connection</span></a></span><span> ? is to keep a standby hose attachment to get a connection from shore or from other ships for onboard firefighting, in case there is a total failure of pumps onboard. While using the International shore connection, the sea water is supplied at a pre-decided pressure and is connected to the ship ? s fire main.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; line-height: 150%;\">\n<strong style=\"mso-bidi-font-weight: normal;\"><span>3.11<span style=\"mso-tab-count: 1;\"> ? ? ? ? </span>Small fires</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<span>Given the nature of fire is that a classification of the different types of fire is performed:</span>\n</p>\n\n</strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>CLASS A FIRE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Fire where ordinary combustible materials such as wood, cloth, paper, rubber and some plastics are involved.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>The water is used for cooling purposes in order to reduce the temperature of the burned material below its ignition temperature.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%285%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; mso-outline-level: 1;\">\n<strong><span>CLASS B FIRE</span></strong>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>Fires involving flammable liquids, greases and gases.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span>The smothering effect of oxygen exclusion is most effective. ? Another method involves removing extinction fuel and temperature reduction.</span>\n</p>\n\n<p style=\"margin-bottom: .0001pt; text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt;\">\n<span><img src=\"@@PLUGINFILE@@/image%20%286%29.png\"></span>\n</p>\n\n</p>\n <table class=\"Tablaconcuadrcula1\" style=\"width: 417.75pt; border-collapse: collapse; border: none; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-yfti-tbllook: 1184; mso-padding-alt: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt;\" border=\"1\" width=\"836\" cellspacing=\"0\" cellpadding=\"0\"> <tbody> <tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 0; mso-yfti-firstrow: yes; height: 20.25pt;\"> <td style=\"width: 89.25pt; border: solid windowtext 1.0pt; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 20.25pt;\" valign=\"top\" nowrap=\"nowrap\" width=\"179\"> \n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 3.0pt .0001pt 3.0pt;\">\n</a><span>Fire</span>\n</p>\n </td> <td style=\"width: 155.25pt; border: solid windowtext 1.0pt; border-left: none; mso-border-left-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 20.25pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"311\"> \n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 3.0pt .0001pt 3.0pt;\">\n<span>Material Types</span>\n</p>\n </td> <td style=\"width: 172.5pt; border: solid windowtext 1.0pt; border-left: none; mso-border-left-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; mso-border-alt: solid windowtext .5pt; padding: 0cm 5.4pt 0cm 5.4pt; height: 20.25pt;\" valign=\"top\" width=\"345\"> \n<p style=\"text-align: justify; line-height: 12.0pt; margin: 0cm 3.0pt .0001pt 3.0pt;\">\n<span>M ? ethod of Extinction</span>\n</p>\n </td> </tr> <tr style=\"mso-yfti-irow: 1; height: 33.0p